{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E5pAEHbWXiW4JD3DFyXY6U","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WIRETAP\n2\t\n3\t*\"Somebody's always listening.\"*\n4\t\n5\tAn original crime game inspired by **The Wire (2002-2008)**. It plays both sides of one West Baltimore corner: a police detail that builds a case with cameras, pagers, payphones and paper, and the crew that runs the corner and adapts to every move the detail makes. It is gritty, slow-burning and human. Nobody gets a clean win.\n6\t\n7\t- **Title:** WIRETAP. I brainstormed 50 riffs on \"The Wire\" and numbered them, then a cryptographic RNG (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50)+1)\"`) drew **7**, which was \"Wiretap\". Keep this title. Don't re-roll it.\n8\t- **File stem** (TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]`, written like the other runs, e.g. `Rewired-TheWire`): `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319`\n9\t- **Deliverables, in this folder (`work/`):**\n10\t  - `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html`: the whole game in one file.\n11\t  - `exit-interview.json`, next to the html (shape in §13).\n12\t  - `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319/`: the dev folder with sources, tools and tests (§3.2).\n13\t- **Readers:** whoever builds this from TASK.md and this plan, possibly another AI model working alone. Every system, number, name and piece of content you need is here. Where you have to make a choice yourself, the plan says so.\n14\t- **How to read it:** §1-§4 and §14 first, then build in §14's order and look up each system (§5-§10) and appendix as you reach it. §17 is the ship checklist.\n15\t- **Shared folder:** other agents share the parent directory. Don't read files outside this `work/` folder, except your own temp dirs and the two GitHub repos named below.\n16\t\n17\t---\n18\t\n19\t## 0. Contents\n20\t\n21\t1. The game on one page\n22\t2. Genre, perspective and why\n23\t3. Tech stack, architecture and modules\n24\t4. Controls and 10-foot UX\n25\t5. Core systems (field, perception, camera, tail, chase, raid)\n26\t6. Investigation systems (board, notebook, cipher, box, paper, wire, brass/heat/legal/case)\n27\t7. The corner (crew side)\n28\t8. Story mode: setting, cast, five episodes, endings\n29\t9. Versus mode and online play (AI Hub)\n30\t10. Attract mode\n31\t11. Audio: music, effects, voices\n32\t12. Art direction and world building\n33\t13. About/Credits screen and the exit interview\n34\t14. Build order and milestones\n35\t15. Risks and de-risking\n36\t16. Testing plan (lightweight)\n37\t17. Definition of done\n38\t- Appendices: A map · B Annex map · C cipher and pages · D wire calls · E interrogation data · F affidavit and judge · G writing guide and key scenes · H music scores · I voice profiles · J data schemas\n39\t\n40\t---\n41\t\n42\t## 1. The game on one page\n43\t\n44\t**Core loop (police, \"the detail\"):** *Watch → identify → intercept → decode → act → answer to the brass → climb the chain.*\n45\tYou plant yourself near the corner without getting \"made\", photograph the crew mid-deal, put faces, names and numbers on the Board, write an affidavit good enough for a judge, then sit in the wire room deciding which calls you're legally allowed to hear. Every arrest you make for the bosses' statistics warns the crew. Every week you wait, the Major's patience runs lower.\n46\t\n47\t**Counter-loop (street, \"the corner\"):** between episodes you play **Terrence \"Tee\" Mabry**, the crew's lieutenant, for one night shift. You post lookouts, keep the line moving, move the stash, page the re-up through the code, spot the van that wasn't there yesterday and call \"5-0\". How well you play Tee changes how hard Dana's next day is. That is the game's thesis: *the game is rigged both ways.*\n48\t\n49\t**Modes:**\n50\t\n51\t| Mode | Players | What it is |\n52\t|---|---|---|\n53\t| **The Case** (story) | 1 | Five episodes, about 60-75 min: field missions, the Annex hub, the wire room, the box, the judge, plus four corner interludes as Tee and a finale with two paths and computed endings |\n54\t| **The Game** (versus) | 1-4 | Corner vs Detail, 5-minute rounds. The detail hides among AI civilians and photographs deals; the corner makes money and calls out cops (SpyParty-style hidden identity). Bots fill empty seats. Local split screen for 2, online through AI Hub shared-world for up to 4 |\n55\t| **Attract** | 0 | Starts after 10 s idle on title/menus: a looping reel of bot-played vignettes from every system. Any input exits |\n56\t\n57\t**Pillars**\n58\t1. **Real over cool.** No superheroes. Police work is waiting, paper and patience. Violence is rare, sudden and has weight. Failure usually doesn't end the game. It makes things worse.\n59\t2. **Both sides of the corner.** One simulation (the CornerSim, §7), seen from both sides. Choices on one side ripple into the other.\n60\t3. **Institutions are the antagonists.** The Major's statistics, the judge's rules, the boss's count. Meters (Brass, Heat, Legal, Case) make those pressures playable.\n61\t4. **Watch, wait, listen.** The best tools are observation and patience: staying unnoticed, reading roles, hearing a key line in a call about chicken wings.\n62\t5. **Couch and TV first.** DualSense on a TV at 10 ft. Large type, few choices per screen, prompts with the right glyphs.\n63\t\n64\t**What the player can do:** walk, run, sneak, blend in, watch and read roles, photograph, eavesdrop, talk, pay informants, read payphone numbers, sit in a van and shoot through a long lens, tail on foot with partner hand-offs, chase on foot (vault fences, tackle, cuff), search for ditched evidence, dig through trash, raid a stash garage, interrogate, decode pager codes, build the Board, write and defend an affidavit, run the wire room (minimize, spot-check, log, identify voices, decode live pages), make institutional choices, and play the other side as Tee.\n65\t**What the player cannot do:** shoot people (Dana never fires her gun, and that is on purpose), drive freely (travel between places is a car-ride dialogue scene), climb buildings, or break the law without the game noticing (Legal meter).\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## 2. Genre, perspective and why\n70\t\n71\t- **Genre:** *stealth investigation / crime drama*, with a light **management sim** (the corner) and an **asymmetric social-stealth versus** mode. The show is about surveillance, institutions and the corner economy. A shooter or beat-'em-up would betray it. Stealth (don't get made), deduction (Board, cipher, affidavit, voices) and management (the count) are the show's verbs. Catalogue genre: **Stealth** (§13).\n72\t- **Perspective:** **isometric 2.5D** (`view: 'iso'`, the engine's yaw 45 / pitch 30) over a 72×56-cell West Baltimore neighborhood. Iso turns rowhouse blocks into a readable diorama, shows sightlines and distance (what tails and surveillance depend on) and fits the engine's stealth tooling (`E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, `r.sightCone`, `cut` walls, x-ray). The camera turns in 90° steps (L1/R1) to see behind buildings.\n73\t  - Interiors (Annex, wire room, the box, judge's chambers) are small TileMaps. The Annex uses iso. The others use `brawler` (pitch 25) for close, theatrical framing.\n74\t  - Investigation screens (Board, wire room panels, affidavit, notebook) are HTML \"case file\" panels over the pixel world. The engine's own guidance for a modern adaptation is `E.Shell`-style HTML.\n75\t- **Look:** the engine's native retro-modern pixel style (PS1-era 2D), pushed toward grit: sodium-orange streetlight, overcast daylight, rain, boarded vacants, Formstone, phone wires overhead (the visual motif for a game called WIRETAP).\n76\t\n77\t---\n78\t\n79\t## 3. Tech stack, architecture and modules\n80\t\n81\t### 3.1 Stack (pin these)\n82\t| Piece | Version | Source | Notes |\n83\t|---|---|---|---|\n84\t| my-3D2dge **full engine** `my-3d2dge.js` | **v0.85.1** (check `E.version`) | `github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` | One file with every module (story, strategy for `E.Clock`, grid, drive). **Never edit it.** Extend from game code |\n85\t| AI Hub session client `session.js` | **1.4.0** | `raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | Paste it **unmodified** as the **first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`** |\n86\t| AI Hub harness | `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | same repo, `lib/hub/` | Test only. Not shipped |\n87\t| Your game code | vanilla JS (ES2020), one IIFE | this plan | No other libraries. No runtime network. No web fonts (system font stacks only) |\n88\t| Build/test | Node ≥ 18, Playwright 1.63 (preinstalled on this machine) | | Engine tools: `tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs` |\n89\t\n90\tFetch fresh copies (don't rely on any planner temp dir):\n91\t```sh\n92\tDEV=Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319\n93\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git /tmp/wt-engine\n94\tgit -C /tmp/wt-engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n95\tmkdir -p $DEV/engine $DEV/vendor $DEV/tests/hub && cp -r /tmp/wt-engine/dist/handoff/* $DEV/engine/\n96\tcurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js -o $DEV/vendor/aihub-session.js\n97\tfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f -o $DEV/tests/hub/$f; done\n98\t```\n99\tRead `engine/API.md` whole first. Then read `engine/AI_GUIDE.md` sections *Standard features*, *Heights*, *Mover*, *Perception*, *Shell*, *Lighting* and *Genre recipes → Third-person stealth*, and `engine/docs/ASSETS.md` before writing shapes. Start from `engine/game/game.js` (the template) and `engine/kits/stealth.js` (guards, Suspicion, E.Shell HUD, layered music).\n100\t\n101\t### 3.2 Dev folder layout\n102\t```\n103\tWiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319/\n104\t  engine/                 the handoff package, unmodified (my-3d2dge.js, tools/, docs, kits)\n105\t  vendor/aihub-session.js the hub client 1.4.0, unmodified\n106\t  src/                    game sources, concatenated in name order inside ONE IIFE by the build\n107\t    00-core.js            E alias, constants, rng streams, event bus, registries, STATE, utils\n108\t    01-settings.js        game settings specs, gamepad axis-inversion wrapper, glyph style\n109\t    02-input.js           WIRETAP input preset, Controller classes, prompt/glyph helpers (canvas + DOM)\n110\t    03-audio.js           songs (App. H), sfx defs, ambience beds, Animalese voice synth, phone filter\n111\t    04-ui.js              DOM UI kit (screens, focus navigation, hover focus, legend footer), headless-safe\n112\t    05-world.js           neighborhood TileMap (App. A), wall types, facade decorators, shapes, lights, weather, time-of-day presets\n113\t    06-people.js          cast roster (rigs, colors, voices), civilian generator, custom poses\n114\t    07-agents.js          Agent (Mover + rig + brain), routines, FlowField cache, interactions, barks\n115\t    08-perception.js      \"made\" system: observers × police suspicion, behavior factors, HUD heat\n116\t    09-corner.js          CornerSim (roles, customers, sales, stash, count, shutdown, heat hooks)\n117\t    10-camera.js          Watch mode, reticle, read/observe, photo scoring, polaroid renderer\n118\t    11-tail.js            tail routes, counter-surveillance, distance band, partner hand-off\n119\t    12-chase.js           escape graph, ditch, vault, tackle, cuff, search\n120\t    13-raid.js            raid planning, breach, interior reactions, stash search\n121\t    14-board.js           case model + Board screen\n122\t    15-notebook.js        notebook screen (objectives, map, people, evidence, phones, case)\n123\t    16-cipher.js          keypad ciphers + decoder UI\n124\t    17-box.js             interrogation system (box + kitchen configs)\n125\t    18-paper.js           affidavit builder + judge cross-examination\n126\t    19-wire.js            wire room (call scheduler, playback, minimization, logging, voice ID, pages)\n127\t    20-meters.js          Brass / Heat / Legal / Case rules and consequences, ops choices\n128\t    21-story.js           Director (episode beats), mission runner, E.Story setup, epigraphs, montage, endings\n129\t    30-data-*.js          content as data: people, missions, dialogue nodes, calls, routes, evidence, credits\n130\t    40-scenes.js          boot, title, attract shell, episode card, annex, field, box, chambers, wire, corner, montage, credits, about, versus, results\n131\t    50-versus.js          versus rules, roles, bots, split screen\n132\t    51-net.js             Transport (Local / Hub), host sim loop, snapshots, inputs, finish\n133\t    52-attract.js         attract reel + bot drivers\n134\t    60-debug.js           game.debug, deep links, ?dev=1 overlay, test hooks\n135\t    99-start.js           new E.Game, scenes, boot steps, start (or AI Hub round)\n136\t    ui.css                HTML UI theme (case-file look)\n137\t    credits.json          About/Credits + exit-interview source of truth (§13)\n138\t  tools/build.mjs         bundles the single html + writes exit-interview.json\n139\t  tests/\n140\t    headless.mjs          systems + smoke tests on the engine's fixed-step clock (Node vm)\n141\t    browser.mjs           Playwright: gamepad glyphs/invert, attract, touch-only notice, screenshots\n142\t    hub/                  harness.html, bridge.js, round.js + hub-check.mjs\n143\t  index.html              dev page: engine + src files as separate scripts (fast iteration)\n144\t```\n145\t\n146\t### 3.3 Single-file bundle (`tools/build.mjs`)\n147\tOrder inside `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html`:\n148\t1. `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n149\t2. `<script>` + `vendor/aihub-session.js` (byte-identical) + `</script>`\n150\t3. viewport meta (`width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover`), hub tags (§9.3), `<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">`, `<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">`, `<title>WIRETAP</title>`, `<style>` with `ui.css`\n151\t4. `<body>`: boot splash markup (run the engine in Node `vm` at build time and insert `My3D2dge.bootSplash('WIRETAP')`), then `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas><div id=\"ui\"></div>`\n152\t5. `<script>` engine `</script>`, then `<script>` game: `(() => { 'use strict'; const CREDITS = <credits.json>; …src files in order… })();`\n153\t\n154\tThe build must:\n155\t- fail if any inlined JS contains `</script` (escape it as `<\\/script`)\n156\t- re-parse `credits.json`\n157\t- write `../exit-interview.json` from `credits.json` (§13) and `JSON.parse` it back to verify\n158\t- print the bundle size (expect about 1.6-1.9 MB)\n159\t\n160\t### 3.4 Architecture rules (non-negotiable; they make attract, bots, network and tests possible)\n161\t1. **Controllers, not input.** Gameplay never reads `game.input` directly. Every controllable character has a `ctl`: `HumanController(input)`, `BotController`, `RemoteController` (network) or a scripted one. A controller exposes `move()`, `aim()`, `down(a)`, `pressed(a)` and `released(a)`. Scenes build the `E.Mover` intent `{ move, sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }` from `ctl`.\n162\t2. **Systems are model + view.** Every system keeps pure state and rules (headless-testable). Drawing (canvas) and DOM views read the state. The UI kit and `E.Shell` are headless-safe: no DOM means no view and the same model.\n163\t3. **Content is data.** People, missions, beats, dialogue nodes, calls, routes, evidence, board slots, ending cards and credits live in `30-data-*.js` objects with documented schemas (App. J). A boot step `validateData()` checks cross-references (missing ids, unknown people, bad cells) and reports them through `game.fail`. That is the \"infrastructure for designer tools\" the task asks for.\n164\t4. **Registries + event bus.** `def(kind, id, spec)` / `get(kind, id)`. `bus.on('photo' | 'made' | 'evidence' | 'beat' | 'sale' | 'callLogged' | …)`. Meters, toasts, achievements-style stats and the debug overlay listen. Systems never call each other's UI.\n165\t5. **Seeded randomness.** Use `E.rng(seed)` streams per system (`rng.world`, `rng.sim`, `rng.cosmetic`), never `Math.random`, in anything that affects play. Versus host streams come from `AIHub.seed` / `AIHub.rng(label)`.\n166\t6. **World units and dt.** Engine rules: 1 tile = 16 units, z up, fixed step 1/120 s, draw only through engine primitives on the game buffer.\n167\t7. **One save state.** `STATE` (App. J) is the only thing `game.saves` writes. Systems rebuild themselves from it.\n168\t8. **Global per-step systems run from a scene wrapper.** Engine timers and tweens end with each scene, so every scene is registered through `withGlobals(scene)`, whose `update` first runs `Globals.tick(dt)`: the idle timer, the attract director, the device/glyph watcher and the hub hooks.\n169\t\n170\t### 3.5 Scenes\n171\t| Scene | View | Pausable | Purpose |\n172\t|---|---|---|---|\n173\t| `title` | iso (live diorama) | no | Logo, main menu, idle timer → attract, touch-only notice |\n174\t| `attract` | varies | no | Demo reel (§10) |\n175\t| `card` | none | no | Episode title + epigraph (black, white text) |\n176\t| `annex` | iso interior | yes | Hub between missions: stations (§6.0) |\n177\t| `field` | iso | yes | All neighborhood missions (photos, tail, chase, van, trash, raid, money tail, cold open, corner interludes as Tee) |\n178\t| `wire` | brawler interior + DOM | yes | Wire room shifts (§6.6) |\n179\t| `box` | brawler interior + DOM | yes | Interrogation / kitchen talk (§6.4) |\n180\t| `chambers` | brawler interior + DOM | yes | Affidavit review with the judge (§6.5) |\n181\t| `montage` | iso/brawler stills | no | Epilogue cards (§8.8) |\n182\t| `credits` | none | no | Credits roll after the finale |\n183\t| `about` | DOM | no | About & Credits pages (§13) |\n184\t| `versusSetup`, `versus`, `versusResults` | iso, split | versus yes (local only) | §9 |\n185\t| `shutdown` | none | no | Detail shut down (game over) → retry from autosave or montage |\n186\t| `hubWait` | none | no | AI Hub only: \"Connecting…\" while `AIHub.connect` resolves (no prompts, no menus) |\n187\t\n188\tThe Board, notebook, cipher, affidavit editor, ops choice and evidence viewer are **UI screens** opened over `annex`/`field`/`wire`, not scenes.\n189\t\n190\t### 3.6 Engine setup (99-start.js)\n191\t```js\n192\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso'], input: KEYS, id: 'wiretap',\n193\t  menu: { items: [['Restart this part', () => Director.restartBeat()], ['Episode select', () => Director.toEpisodes()]],\n194\t          settings: GAME_SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title', saves: true } });   // Save/Load in the pause menu; saves.canSave only in the Annex\n195\tE.style.propSize = 1.35;                       // props match size-1.25 heroes\n196\tinstallGamepadInversion(game);                  // before the first tick (§4.4)\n197\tconst ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: THEME, meters: .0625 });   // HUD, toasts, standard pause/settings/controls; 16 units = 1 m\n198\tgame.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'WIRETAP', version: '1.0', steps: BOOT_STEPS, messages: BOOT_LINES } });\n199\t```\n200\t- **Boot steps:** \"Laying Ridley Avenue\" (map, FlowField cache), \"Hanging the phone lines\" (wires, shapes), \"Waking the block\" (roster/rigs), \"Checking the paperwork\" (`validateData`), \"Charging the pagers\" (audio defs).\n201\t- **Boot lines** (the engine's flavor messages): \"Pouring the coffee\", \"Juking the stats\", \"Filling out the 24-hour report\", \"Finding a parking space on Ridley\".\n202\t- **In an AI Hub session** (`AIHub.active`), 99-start skips the title: `menu.quit = false`, `autoPause: false`, and §9.3's async flow calls `game.start` itself and goes straight into the versus round. The `game.start` call above is the non-session path.\n203\t\n204\t---\n205\t\n206\t## 4. Controls and 10-foot UX\n207\t\n208\t### 4.1 Input preset `KEYS` (custom; include moves and the menu keys verbatim, since custom presets don't inherit them)\n209\t```js\n210\tconst KEYS = {\n211\t  up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'],\n212\t  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n213\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','KeyJ','KeyZ','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','KeyX','KeyK','Pad1'],\n214\t  interact: ['KeyE','Pad0'], crouch: ['KeyC','ControlLeft','Pad1'], blend: ['KeyF','Pad2'], partner: ['KeyQ','Pad3'],\n215\t  notebook: ['Tab','KeyN','Pad8'], map: ['KeyM'],\n216\t  watch: ['Mouse2','Pad6'], photo: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], sprint: ['ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad10'],\n217\t  rotL: ['BracketLeft','Pad4'], rotR: ['BracketRight','Pad5'], camReset: ['KeyR','Pad11'],\n218\t  tabPrev: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabNext: ['KeyE','Pad5'], uiAlt: ['Digit2','KeyT','Pad2'], uiInfo: ['Digit3','KeyI','Pad3'], uiFast: ['ShiftLeft','Pad7'],\n219\t  boxTop: ['Digit1','Pad3'], boxLeft: ['Digit2','Pad2'], boxRight: ['Digit3','Pad1'], boxBottom: ['Digit4','Pad0']   // the box's diamond (§6.4)\n220\t};\n221\t```\n222\tOn the Controls screen, hide `tabPrev`, `tabNext`, `uiAlt`, `uiInfo`, `uiFast` and the four `box*` actions with `menu.controls: { tabPrev: false, … }`, and name the rest (`interact: 'Interact / vault / tackle'`, `blend: 'Act natural (hold)'`, `partner: 'Partner / crew orders'`, `watch: 'Watch (aim camera)'`, `photo: 'Take photo'`). Pad8 (Create) opens the notebook **on release, and only if Options (Pad9) wasn't pressed during the hold**, because Create+Options belongs to the AI Hub. While Pad8 is held in a session, call `input.suppress('pause')`. Don't use the touchpad button (Pad17): the engine has no label for it and not every browser reports it.\n223\t\n224\t### 4.2 Context maps (DualSense / keyboard + mouse)\n225\t| Context | DualSense | Keyboard + mouse |\n226\t|---|---|---|\n227\t| Move / sneak | L-stick (analog: walk) · ○ crouch toggle | WASD/arrows · C or Ctrl |\n228\t| Run | L3 toggles run · (chase/tail: run becomes sprint, with stamina) | hold Shift |\n229\t| Act natural (blend) | hold □ | hold F |\n230\t| Interact / vault / tackle / cuff | ✕ (context) | E · or left-click the highlighted thing in the world (walks there if far) |\n231\t| Watch (aim camera) | hold L2, aim with R-stick | hold right mouse, aim with the mouse |\n232\t| Photo | R2 while watching | left click while watching |\n233\t| Look ahead | R-stick (when not watching): camera peeks up to 64 px | move mouse toward a screen edge (edge peek, optional setting) |\n234\t| Rotate view / reset | L1 / R1 · R3 | [ / ] · R |\n235\t| Zoom | (engine) via Settings · D-pad ↑/↓ is movement, not zoom | wheel, - / = |\n236\t| Partner (hand off the eye, commands) / crew orders as Tee | △ (Tee: hold △ for the order wheel) | Q (Tee: hold Q, wheel follows the mouse) |\n237\t| Notebook / map | Create | Tab (or N) / M |\n238\t| Pause | Options | Esc / P |\n239\t| UI screens | D-pad/L-stick move focus · ✕ select · ○ back · □ alt · △ info · L1/R1 tabs · R2 hold fast | arrows/WASD · Enter/Space · Esc/Backspace · 2 / T · 3 / I · Q / E · Shift · **hover focuses, click selects, wheel scrolls** |\n240\t| Dialogue choices | ✕ on highlighted · face-button shortcuts when the choice shows a glyph (△ □ ○ ✕ diamond in the box, §6.4) | arrows + Enter, number keys 1-4, hover + click |\n241\t\n242\tSettings add **Run: Hold / Toggle** and **Watch: Hold / Toggle**.\n243\t\n244\t### 4.3 Prompts and glyphs\n245\t- **Source of truth:** `game.input.label(action)` (the player's bindings, `lastDevice`, `glyphs`). It returns, for example, `'✕' '○' '□' '△' 'L1' 'R2' 'Create' 'Options' 'L3' 'R3'` (PlayStation), `'A' 'B' 'X' 'Y' 'LB' 'RT' 'View' 'Menu' 'LS' 'RS'` (Xbox), and `'E' 'Shift' 'Tab' 'Space' 'Left click' 'Right click'` (keyboard/mouse).\n246\t- **Canvas glyphs** (`Glyph.draw(g, label, x, y)` in 02-input.js), 9×9 pixel art:\n247\t  - PlayStation: disc with symbol. ✕ `#7aa7ff` · ○ `#ff6b6b` · □ `#f08fd8` · △ `#45d6a0`.\n248\t  - Xbox: disc with letter. A `#5dc24a` · B `#e8483c` · X `#3d8ef0` · Y `#f2c230`.\n249\t  - Shoulders and triggers: a rounded pill with text (`L1`, `R2`, `LB`, `RT`). Create/Options/View/Menu: a small pill.\n250\t  - Keys: a keycap box with `E.font` text. Mouse: a 7×11 mouse sprite with the pressed button lit.\n251\t- **DOM glyphs** (`<span class=\"g g-ps-cross\">✕</span>`, `<kbd>E</kbd>`, `<span class=\"g g-mouse-l\"></span>`) use the same colors in CSS. Style E.Shell's key chips (`.esh-k`, `.esh-pr`, `.esh-keys`) with an override stylesheet so its prompts show the same colored glyphs. Don't hide the `esh-` CSS. Map label text to classes.\n252\t- **Follow the last device.** Every frame, compare `input.lastDevice` (plus `padStyle()` and the glyph setting) with last frame's. On change, re-render DOM legends and prompts. Add hysteresis: ignore mouse moves under 6 px so a nudged mouse doesn't flip a pad player's prompts.\n253\t- **Defaults:** PlayStation glyphs. Settings → *Button symbols: PlayStation / Xbox*. Override the standard `glyphs` setting by defining it again in `GAME_SETTINGS` with `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Game', label: 'Button symbols', options: [['playstation','PlayStation  ✕ ○ □ △'],['xbox','Xbox  A B X Y']], value: 'playstation', apply: (g, v) => g.players.forEach(p => p.glyphs = v) }`. Verify it now appears on the Settings screen. If redefining a standard key misbehaves, use a new key `padSymbols` that also calls `game.settings.set('glyphs', v)`.\n254\t\n255\t### 4.4 Gamepad axis inversion (the ~10% inverted-input issue)\n256\tSettings (group *Game*): **Invert stick left/right** (`stickInvX`) and **Invert stick up/down** (`stickInvY`). Don't reuse the key `invertY`: the engine already has that one, and it only inverts the aim stick. They flip both sticks' axes for every pad and every consumer (gameplay, engine menus, E.Shell, versus) through one wrapper installed before the first `input.tick`:\n257\t```js\n258\tfunction installGamepadInversion(game) {\n259\t  const real = navigator.getGamepads ? navigator.getGamepads.bind(navigator) : () => [];\n260\t  const wrap = p => { if (!p) return p; const ix = game.settings.stickInvX, iy = game.settings.stickInvY; if (!ix && !iy) return p;\n261\t    const a = Array.from(p.axes); if (ix) { a[0] = -a[0]; if (a.length > 2) a[2] = -a[2]; } if (iy) { a[1] = -a[1]; if (a.length > 3) a[3] = -a[3]; }\n262\t    return { id: p.id, index: p.index, connected: p.connected, mapping: p.mapping, timestamp: p.timestamp, buttons: p.buttons, axes: a,\n263\t             vibrationActuator: p.vibrationActuator, hapticActuators: p.hapticActuators }; };\n264\t  try { Object.defineProperty(navigator, 'getGamepads', { configurable: true, value: () => Array.from(real() || []).map(wrap) }); } catch (e) { game.fail('gamepad inversion', e); }\n265\t}\n266\t```\n267\tHelp text: \"Flips the sticks for controllers that report them backwards.\" Test it with a fake gamepad (§16).\n268\t\n269\t### 4.5 10-foot UI rules\n270\t- **Type.** HTML body `font-size: clamp(18px, 2.6vh, 34px)` (about 28 px at 1080p), headings 1.6-2.4×. Canvas text uses the engine's 5×7 font at `res 'ps1'` (about 28 px glyphs at 1080p) for dialogue and barks. Never below that.\n271\t- **Density.** At most 6 focusable items in a list, at most 8 cards in a grid page, no tables of data, one primary action per screen. Long content pages with L1/R1.\n272\t- **Thumbnail test.** Scale a 1920×1080 screenshot to 480×270. If any essential text is unreadable there, it's too small or dense.\n273\t- **Safe area.** 5% margins on all screen-anchored UI (TV overscan). Keep the top-right 110×60 CSS px clear for the engine's corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`).\n274\t- **Focus.** 4 px amber `#ffb347` outline, 1.04 scale and a soft tick sound. Spatial navigation: nearest focusable in the pressed direction, by rect centers with an angle penalty. **Hover focuses** (pointerover) and click activates.\n275\t- **Hover patch.** Add one global handler so hover moves focus in the engine's own E.Shell screens too: `document.addEventListener('pointerover', e => { if (e.pointerType !== 'mouse') return; const b = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('.esh-sh button, .esh-sh [tabindex], .esh-sh select, .esh-sh input'); if (b) b.focus({ preventScroll: true }); });` E.Shell navigates by `document.activeElement`, so this keeps them in sync.\n276\t- **Legend.** Every screen has a footer legend built from actions (`UI.legend([['confirm','Select'],['cancel','Back'],['tabNext','Next page']])`) that re-renders on device change.\n277\t- **Same game on every device.** Keyboard+mouse and pad use the same screens and layouts. Only prompts differ. No desktop-only layout.\n278\t- **Color is never the only signal.** Suspicion uses icons (`·` `?` `!`) plus meters. Pertinent/non-pertinent uses icons plus text.\n279\t\n280\t### 4.6 HUD (E.Shell `ui.hud` each step; field scenes)\n281\t- `objective: { step: 'EP1 · 2/4', title, text, at, from: dana }`. It shows distance and above/below (the Shell was built with `meters: .0625`, so 16 units = 1 m).\n282\t- `status`: `Unnoticed` / `Noticed` / `Watched` / `MADE` / `Blending` / `In the van` / `Winded`, with a color *and* a word.\n283\t- `bars`: **Heat** (max suspicion toward the player 0..1, label \"Eyes on you\"), **Stamina** (chase/tail), **Distance** (tail, §5.5).\n284\t- `counts`: `[['Film', 27], ['Fund', '$180']]`.\n285\t- `prompt: { act: 'interact', label: 'Talk', hint: 'Pockets' }` for the nearest/hovered interactable (§5.1).\n286\t- `minimap: { map, at: dana, markers }` (objective, known payphones, last-seen target).\n287\t- `ui.toast(title, text)` for photos (\"Terrence 'Tee' Mabry ★★☆ ACT\"), numbers, evidence and meter changes. `ui.tutorial(title, text)` for first-time tips (once each, stored in STATE.tips).\n288\t- Over each watcher's head in the world: `·` (calm, only with the assist on), `?` (noticed, amber), `!` (made, red), with a 12-px suspicion bar. Same as the stealth kit's overlay.\n289\t\n290\t### 4.7 Settings (game group; the engine adds Display/Sound/Comfort/Controls)\n291\t| Key | Label | Values (default first) |\n292\t|---|---|---|\n293\t| `glyphs` | Button symbols | PlayStation, Xbox |\n294\t| `stickInvX` / `stickInvY` | Invert stick left/right · up/down | off |\n295\t| `difficulty` | Case difficulty | Detective, Rookie, Murder Police |\n296\t| `runMode` / `watchMode` | Run · Watch | Hold, Toggle |\n297\t| `textSpeed` | Text speed | Normal (45 cps), Slow (28), Fast (80), Instant |\n298\t| `voices` | Voice volume | slider .8 (multiplies the voice bus) |\n299\t| `language` | Strong language | Uncensored, Bleeped (replaces a small word list with `—`) |\n300\t| `hints` | Investigation hints | on: highlights key wire lines on Rookie, Szabo hints after 2 failed tries |\n301\t| `cones` | Show sight cones | on (assist; `r.sightCone` for watchers when you're near) |\n302\t| `edgePeek` | Mouse edge peek | on |\n303\t\n304\tDifficulty multiplies suspicion rise (Rookie .7 · Detective 1 · Murder Police 1.35), the minimization window (10 s · 8 s · 6 s), Brass decay (.7 · 1 · 1.3) and tail tolerance (lost after 16 s · 12 s · 9 s).\n305\t\n306\t### 4.8 Touch-only devices\n307\t- **Detection** at boot and on every input: `touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, and no gamepad connected, and no keydown seen yet.\n308\t- **What shows:** the title shows a centered panel, \"**Controller or keyboard required**\" (subtext: \"Connect a controller or keyboard to play. The demo will run in the meantime.\"), and a large **Settings** button. Tap it → `game.menu('settings')`.\n309\t- **No touch controls anywhere:** never pass `touch:` in scenes, set `game.input.touchEnabled = false`, and `game.settings.set('touch', 'off')` on touch-only devices.\n310\t- **Attract still runs:** after 10 s it starts, and a tap returns to the panel.\n311\t- **Recovery:** `gamepadconnected` or any `keydown` hides the panel and shows the normal menu.\n312\t\n313\t---\n314\t\n315\t## 5. Core systems\n316\t\n317\t### 5.0 Shared numbers\n318\t| Thing | Value |\n319\t|---|---|\n320\t| Tile | 16 units; map 72×56 cells = 1152×896 units |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5584c513-9280-4b46-aaca-c597f867a104","uuid":"b5106107-6e7b-4cc9-a5c5-20f69652583a","timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:58:30.578Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-125821__x600c07/work/PLAN.md","content":"# WIRETAP\n\n*\"Somebody's always listening.\"*\n\nAn original crime game inspired by **The Wire (2002-2008)**. It plays both sides of one West Baltimore corner: a police detail that builds a case with cameras, pagers, payphones and paper, and the crew that runs the corner and adapts to every move the detail makes. It is gritty, slow-burning and human. Nobody gets a clean win.\n\n- **Title:** WIRETAP. I brainstormed 50 riffs on \"The Wire\" and numbered them, then a cryptographic RNG (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50)+1)\"`) drew **7**, which was \"Wiretap\". Keep this title. Don't re-roll it.\n- **File stem** (TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]`, written like the other runs, e.g. `Rewired-TheWire`): `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319`\n- **Deliverables, in this folder (`work/`):**\n  - `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html`: the whole game in one file.\n  - `exit-interview.json`, next to the html (shape in §13).\n  - `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319/`: the dev folder with sources, tools and tests (§3.2).\n- **Readers:** whoever builds this from TASK.md and this plan, possibly another AI model working alone. Every system, number, name and piece of content you need is here. Where you have to make a choice yourself, the plan says so.\n- **How to read it:** §1-§4 and §14 first, then build in §14's order and look up each system (§5-§10) and appendix as you reach it. §17 is the ship checklist.\n- **Shared folder:** other agents share the parent directory. Don't read files outside this `work/` folder, except your own temp dirs and the two GitHub repos named below.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Contents\n\n1. The game on one page\n2. Genre, perspective and why\n3. Tech stack, architecture and modules\n4. Controls and 10-foot UX\n5. Core systems (field, perception, camera, tail, chase, raid)\n6. Investigation systems (board, notebook, cipher, box, paper, wire, brass/heat/legal/case)\n7. The corner (crew side)\n8. Story mode: setting, cast, five episodes, endings\n9. Versus mode and online play (AI Hub)\n10. Attract mode\n11. Audio: music, effects, voices\n12. Art direction and world building\n13. About/Credits screen and the exit interview\n14. Build order and milestones\n15. Risks and de-risking\n16. Testing plan (lightweight)\n17. Definition of done\n- Appendices: A map · B Annex map · C cipher and pages · D wire calls · E interrogation data · F affidavit and judge · G writing guide and key scenes · H music scores · I voice profiles · J data schemas\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Core loop (police, \"the detail\"):** *Watch → identify → intercept → decode → act → answer to the brass → climb the chain.*\nYou plant yourself near the corner without getting \"made\", photograph the crew mid-deal, put faces, names and numbers on the Board, write an affidavit good enough for a judge, then sit in the wire room deciding which calls you're legally allowed to hear. Every arrest you make for the bosses' statistics warns the crew. Every week you wait, the Major's patience runs lower.\n\n**Counter-loop (street, \"the corner\"):** between episodes you play **Terrence \"Tee\" Mabry**, the crew's lieutenant, for one night shift. You post lookouts, keep the line moving, move the stash, page the re-up through the code, spot the van that wasn't there yesterday and call \"5-0\". How well you play Tee changes how hard Dana's next day is. That is the game's thesis: *the game is rigged both ways.*\n\n**Modes:**\n\n| Mode | Players | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| **The Case** (story) | 1 | Five episodes, about 60-75 min: field missions, the Annex hub, the wire room, the box, the judge, plus four corner interludes as Tee and a finale with two paths and computed endings |\n| **The Game** (versus) | 1-4 | Corner vs Detail, 5-minute rounds. The detail hides among AI civilians and photographs deals; the corner makes money and calls out cops (SpyParty-style hidden identity). Bots fill empty seats. Local split screen for 2, online through AI Hub shared-world for up to 4 |\n| **Attract** | 0 | Starts after 10 s idle on title/menus: a looping reel of bot-played vignettes from every system. Any input exits |\n\n**Pillars**\n1. **Real over cool.** No superheroes. Police work is waiting, paper and patience. Violence is rare, sudden and has weight. Failure usually doesn't end the game. It makes things worse.\n2. **Both sides of the corner.** One simulation (the CornerSim, §7), seen from both sides. Choices on one side ripple into the other.\n3. **Institutions are the antagonists.** The Major's statistics, the judge's rules, the boss's count. Meters (Brass, Heat, Legal, Case) make those pressures playable.\n4. **Watch, wait, listen.** The best tools are observation and patience: staying unnoticed, reading roles, hearing a key line in a call about chicken wings.\n5. **Couch and TV first.** DualSense on a TV at 10 ft. Large type, few choices per screen, prompts with the right glyphs.\n\n**What the player can do:** walk, run, sneak, blend in, watch and read roles, photograph, eavesdrop, talk, pay informants, read payphone numbers, sit in a van and shoot through a long lens, tail on foot with partner hand-offs, chase on foot (vault fences, tackle, cuff), search for ditched evidence, dig through trash, raid a stash garage, interrogate, decode pager codes, build the Board, write and defend an affidavit, run the wire room (minimize, spot-check, log, identify voices, decode live pages), make institutional choices, and play the other side as Tee.\n**What the player cannot do:** shoot people (Dana never fires her gun, and that is on purpose), drive freely (travel between places is a car-ride dialogue scene), climb buildings, or break the law without the game noticing (Legal meter).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Genre, perspective and why\n\n- **Genre:** *stealth investigation / crime drama*, with a light **management sim** (the corner) and an **asymmetric social-stealth versus** mode. The show is about surveillance, institutions and the corner economy. A shooter or beat-'em-up would betray it. Stealth (don't get made), deduction (Board, cipher, affidavit, voices) and management (the count) are the show's verbs. Catalogue genre: **Stealth** (§13).\n- **Perspective:** **isometric 2.5D** (`view: 'iso'`, the engine's yaw 45 / pitch 30) over a 72×56-cell West Baltimore neighborhood. Iso turns rowhouse blocks into a readable diorama, shows sightlines and distance (what tails and surveillance depend on) and fits the engine's stealth tooling (`E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, `r.sightCone`, `cut` walls, x-ray). The camera turns in 90° steps (L1/R1) to see behind buildings.\n  - Interiors (Annex, wire room, the box, judge's chambers) are small TileMaps. The Annex uses iso. The others use `brawler` (pitch 25) for close, theatrical framing.\n  - Investigation screens (Board, wire room panels, affidavit, notebook) are HTML \"case file\" panels over the pixel world. The engine's own guidance for a modern adaptation is `E.Shell`-style HTML.\n- **Look:** the engine's native retro-modern pixel style (PS1-era 2D), pushed toward grit: sodium-orange streetlight, overcast daylight, rain, boarded vacants, Formstone, phone wires overhead (the visual motif for a game called WIRETAP).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack, architecture and modules\n\n### 3.1 Stack (pin these)\n| Piece | Version | Source | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| my-3D2dge **full engine** `my-3d2dge.js` | **v0.85.1** (check `E.version`) | `github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff` | One file with every module (story, strategy for `E.Clock`, grid, drive). **Never edit it.** Extend from game code |\n| AI Hub session client `session.js` | **1.4.0** | `raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | Paste it **unmodified** as the **first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`** |\n| AI Hub harness | `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | same repo, `lib/hub/` | Test only. Not shipped |\n| Your game code | vanilla JS (ES2020), one IIFE | this plan | No other libraries. No runtime network. No web fonts (system font stacks only) |\n| Build/test | Node ≥ 18, Playwright 1.63 (preinstalled on this machine) | | Engine tools: `tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs` |\n\nFetch fresh copies (don't rely on any planner temp dir):\n```sh\nDEV=Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git /tmp/wt-engine\ngit -C /tmp/wt-engine sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\nmkdir -p $DEV/engine $DEV/vendor $DEV/tests/hub && cp -r /tmp/wt-engine/dist/handoff/* $DEV/engine/\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js -o $DEV/vendor/aihub-session.js\nfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js; do curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f -o $DEV/tests/hub/$f; done\n```\nRead `engine/API.md` whole first. Then read `engine/AI_GUIDE.md` sections *Standard features*, *Heights*, *Mover*, *Perception*, *Shell*, *Lighting* and *Genre recipes → Third-person stealth*, and `engine/docs/ASSETS.md` before writing shapes. Start from `engine/game/game.js` (the template) and `engine/kits/stealth.js` (guards, Suspicion, E.Shell HUD, layered music).\n\n### 3.2 Dev folder layout\n```\nWiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319/\n  engine/                 the handoff package, unmodified (my-3d2dge.js, tools/, docs, kits)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js the hub client 1.4.0, unmodified\n  src/                    game sources, concatenated in name order inside ONE IIFE by the build\n    00-core.js            E alias, constants, rng streams, event bus, registries, STATE, utils\n    01-settings.js        game settings specs, gamepad axis-inversion wrapper, glyph style\n    02-input.js           WIRETAP input preset, Controller classes, prompt/glyph helpers (canvas + DOM)\n    03-audio.js           songs (App. H), sfx defs, ambience beds, Animalese voice synth, phone filter\n    04-ui.js              DOM UI kit (screens, focus navigation, hover focus, legend footer), headless-safe\n    05-world.js           neighborhood TileMap (App. A), wall types, facade decorators, shapes, lights, weather, time-of-day presets\n    06-people.js          cast roster (rigs, colors, voices), civilian generator, custom poses\n    07-agents.js          Agent (Mover + rig + brain), routines, FlowField cache, interactions, barks\n    08-perception.js      \"made\" system: observers × police suspicion, behavior factors, HUD heat\n    09-corner.js          CornerSim (roles, customers, sales, stash, count, shutdown, heat hooks)\n    10-camera.js          Watch mode, reticle, read/observe, photo scoring, polaroid renderer\n    11-tail.js            tail routes, counter-surveillance, distance band, partner hand-off\n    12-chase.js           escape graph, ditch, vault, tackle, cuff, search\n    13-raid.js            raid planning, breach, interior reactions, stash search\n    14-board.js           case model + Board screen\n    15-notebook.js        notebook screen (objectives, map, people, evidence, phones, case)\n    16-cipher.js          keypad ciphers + decoder UI\n    17-box.js             interrogation system (box + kitchen configs)\n    18-paper.js           affidavit builder + judge cross-examination\n    19-wire.js            wire room (call scheduler, playback, minimization, logging, voice ID, pages)\n    20-meters.js          Brass / Heat / Legal / Case rules and consequences, ops choices\n    21-story.js           Director (episode beats), mission runner, E.Story setup, epigraphs, montage, endings\n    30-data-*.js          content as data: people, missions, dialogue nodes, calls, routes, evidence, credits\n    40-scenes.js          boot, title, attract shell, episode card, annex, field, box, chambers, wire, corner, montage, credits, about, versus, results\n    50-versus.js          versus rules, roles, bots, split screen\n    51-net.js             Transport (Local / Hub), host sim loop, snapshots, inputs, finish\n    52-attract.js         attract reel + bot drivers\n    60-debug.js           game.debug, deep links, ?dev=1 overlay, test hooks\n    99-start.js           new E.Game, scenes, boot steps, start (or AI Hub round)\n    ui.css                HTML UI theme (case-file look)\n    credits.json          About/Credits + exit-interview source of truth (§13)\n  tools/build.mjs         bundles the single html + writes exit-interview.json\n  tests/\n    headless.mjs          systems + smoke tests on the engine's fixed-step clock (Node vm)\n    browser.mjs           Playwright: gamepad glyphs/invert, attract, touch-only notice, screenshots\n    hub/                  harness.html, bridge.js, round.js + hub-check.mjs\n  index.html              dev page: engine + src files as separate scripts (fast iteration)\n```\n\n### 3.3 Single-file bundle (`tools/build.mjs`)\nOrder inside `Wiretap-TheWire-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-105319.html`:\n1. `<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n2. `<script>` + `vendor/aihub-session.js` (byte-identical) + `</script>`\n3. viewport meta (`width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover`), hub tags (§9.3), `<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">`, `<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">`, `<title>WIRETAP</title>`, `<style>` with `ui.css`\n4. `<body>`: boot splash markup (run the engine in Node `vm` at build time and insert `My3D2dge.bootSplash('WIRETAP')`), then `<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas><div id=\"ui\"></div>`\n5. `<script>` engine `</script>`, then `<script>` game: `(() => { 'use strict'; const CREDITS = <credits.json>; …src files in order… })();`\n\nThe build must:\n- fail if any inlined JS contains `</script` (escape it as `<\\/script`)\n- re-parse `credits.json`\n- write `../exit-interview.json` from `credits.json` (§13) and `JSON.parse` it back to verify\n- print the bundle size (expect about 1.6-1.9 MB)\n\n### 3.4 Architecture rules (non-negotiable; they make attract, bots, network and tests possible)\n1. **Controllers, not input.** Gameplay never reads `game.input` directly. Every controllable character has a `ctl`: `HumanController(input)`, `BotController`, `RemoteController` (network) or a scripted one. A controller exposes `move()`, `aim()`, `down(a)`, `pressed(a)` and `released(a)`. Scenes build the `E.Mover` intent `{ move, sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }` from `ctl`.\n2. **Systems are model + view.** Every system keeps pure state and rules (headless-testable). Drawing (canvas) and DOM views read the state. The UI kit and `E.Shell` are headless-safe: no DOM means no view and the same model.\n3. **Content is data.** People, missions, beats, dialogue nodes, calls, routes, evidence, board slots, ending cards and credits live in `30-data-*.js` objects with documented schemas (App. J). A boot step `validateData()` checks cross-references (missing ids, unknown people, bad cells) and reports them through `game.fail`. That is the \"infrastructure for designer tools\" the task asks for.\n4. **Registries + event bus.** `def(kind, id, spec)` / `get(kind, id)`. `bus.on('photo' | 'made' | 'evidence' | 'beat' | 'sale' | 'callLogged' | …)`. Meters, toasts, achievements-style stats and the debug overlay listen. Systems never call each other's UI.\n5. **Seeded randomness.** Use `E.rng(seed)` streams per system (`rng.world`, `rng.sim`, `rng.cosmetic`), never `Math.random`, in anything that affects play. Versus host streams come from `AIHub.seed` / `AIHub.rng(label)`.\n6. **World units and dt.** Engine rules: 1 tile = 16 units, z up, fixed step 1/120 s, draw only through engine primitives on the game buffer.\n7. **One save state.** `STATE` (App. J) is the only thing `game.saves` writes. Systems rebuild themselves from it.\n8. **Global per-step systems run from a scene wrapper.** Engine timers and tweens end with each scene, so every scene is registered through `withGlobals(scene)`, whose `update` first runs `Globals.tick(dt)`: the idle timer, the attract director, the device/glyph watcher and the hub hooks.\n\n### 3.5 Scenes\n| Scene | View | Pausable | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `title` | iso (live diorama) | no | Logo, main menu, idle timer → attract, touch-only notice |\n| `attract` | varies | no | Demo reel (§10) |\n| `card` | none | no | Episode title + epigraph (black, white text) |\n| `annex` | iso interior | yes | Hub between missions: stations (§6.0) |\n| `field` | iso | yes | All neighborhood missions (photos, tail, chase, van, trash, raid, money tail, cold open, corner interludes as Tee) |\n| `wire` | brawler interior + DOM | yes | Wire room shifts (§6.6) |\n| `box` | brawler interior + DOM | yes | Interrogation / kitchen talk (§6.4) |\n| `chambers` | brawler interior + DOM | yes | Affidavit review with the judge (§6.5) |\n| `montage` | iso/brawler stills | no | Epilogue cards (§8.8) |\n| `credits` | none | no | Credits roll after the finale |\n| `about` | DOM | no | About & Credits pages (§13) |\n| `versusSetup`, `versus`, `versusResults` | iso, split | versus yes (local only) | §9 |\n| `shutdown` | none | no | Detail shut down (game over) → retry from autosave or montage |\n| `hubWait` | none | no | AI Hub only: \"Connecting…\" while `AIHub.connect` resolves (no prompts, no menus) |\n\nThe Board, notebook, cipher, affidavit editor, ops choice and evidence viewer are **UI screens** opened over `annex`/`field`/`wire`, not scenes.\n\n### 3.6 Engine setup (99-start.js)\n```js\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso'], input: KEYS, id: 'wiretap',\n  menu: { items: [['Restart this part', () => Director.restartBeat()], ['Episode select', () => Director.toEpisodes()]],\n          settings: GAME_SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title', saves: true } });   // Save/Load in the pause menu; saves.canSave only in the Annex\nE.style.propSize = 1.35;                       // props match size-1.25 heroes\ninstallGamepadInversion(game);                  // before the first tick (§4.4)\nconst ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: THEME, meters: .0625 });   // HUD, toasts, standard pause/settings/controls; 16 units = 1 m\ngame.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot: { title: 'WIRETAP', version: '1.0', steps: BOOT_STEPS, messages: BOOT_LINES } });\n```\n- **Boot steps:** \"Laying Ridley Avenue\" (map, FlowField cache), \"Hanging the phone lines\" (wires, shapes), \"Waking the block\" (roster/rigs), \"Checking the paperwork\" (`validateData`), \"Charging the pagers\" (audio defs).\n- **Boot lines** (the engine's flavor messages): \"Pouring the coffee\", \"Juking the stats\", \"Filling out the 24-hour report\", \"Finding a parking space on Ridley\".\n- **In an AI Hub session** (`AIHub.active`), 99-start skips the title: `menu.quit = false`, `autoPause: false`, and §9.3's async flow calls `game.start` itself and goes straight into the versus round. The `game.start` call above is the non-session path.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls and 10-foot UX\n\n### 4.1 Input preset `KEYS` (custom; include moves and the menu keys verbatim, since custom presets don't inherit them)\n```js\nconst KEYS = {\n  up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'],\n  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','KeyJ','KeyZ','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','KeyX','KeyK','Pad1'],\n  interact: ['KeyE','Pad0'], crouch: ['KeyC','ControlLeft','Pad1'], blend: ['KeyF','Pad2'], partner: ['KeyQ','Pad3'],\n  notebook: ['Tab','KeyN','Pad8'], map: ['KeyM'],\n  watch: ['Mouse2','Pad6'], photo: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], sprint: ['ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad10'],\n  rotL: ['BracketLeft','Pad4'], rotR: ['BracketRight','Pad5'], camReset: ['KeyR','Pad11'],\n  tabPrev: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabNext: ['KeyE','Pad5'], uiAlt: ['Digit2','KeyT','Pad2'], uiInfo: ['Digit3','KeyI','Pad3'], uiFast: ['ShiftLeft','Pad7'],\n  boxTop: ['Digit1','Pad3'], boxLeft: ['Digit2','Pad2'], boxRight: ['Digit3','Pad1'], boxBottom: ['Digit4','Pad0']   // the box's diamond (§6.4)\n};\n```\nOn the Controls screen, hide `tabPrev`, `tabNext`, `uiAlt`, `uiInfo`, `uiFast` and the four `box*` actions with `menu.controls: { tabPrev: false, … }`, and name the rest (`interact: 'Interact / vault / tackle'`, `blend: 'Act natural (hold)'`, `partner: 'Partner / crew orders'`, `watch: 'Watch (aim camera)'`, `photo: 'Take photo'`). Pad8 (Create) opens the notebook **on release, and only if Options (Pad9) wasn't pressed during the hold**, because Create+Options belongs to the AI Hub. While Pad8 is held in a session, call `input.suppress('pause')`. Don't use the touchpad button (Pad17): the engine has no label for it and not every browser reports it.\n\n### 4.2 Context maps (DualSense / keyboard + mouse)\n| Context | DualSense | Keyboard + mouse |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move / sneak | L-stick (analog: walk) · ○ crouch toggle | WASD/arrows · C or Ctrl |\n| Run | L3 toggles run · (chase/tail: run becomes sprint, with stamina) | hold Shift |\n| Act natural (blend) | hold □ | hold F |\n| Interact / vault / tackle / cuff | ✕ (context) | E · or left-click the highlighted thing in the world (walks there if far) |\n| Watch (aim camera) | hold L2, aim with R-stick | hold right mouse, aim with the mouse |\n| Photo | R2 while watching | left click while watching |\n| Look ahead | R-stick (when not watching): camera peeks up to 64 px | move mouse toward a screen edge (edge peek, optional setting) |\n| Rotate view / reset | L1 / R1 · R3 | [ / ] · R |\n| Zoom | (engine) via Settings · D-pad ↑/↓ is movement, not zoom | wheel, - / = |\n| Partner (hand off the eye, commands) / crew orders as Tee | △ (Tee: hold △ for the order wheel) | Q (Tee: hold Q, wheel follows the mouse) |\n| Notebook / map | Create | Tab (or N) / M |\n| Pause | Options | Esc / P |\n| UI screens | D-pad/L-stick move focus · ✕ select · ○ back · □ alt · △ info · L1/R1 tabs · R2 hold fast | arrows/WASD · Enter/Space · Esc/Backspace · 2 / T · 3 / I · Q / E · Shift · **hover focuses, click selects, wheel scrolls** |\n| Dialogue choices | ✕ on highlighted · face-button shortcuts when the choice shows a glyph (△ □ ○ ✕ diamond in the box, §6.4) | arrows + Enter, number keys 1-4, hover + click |\n\nSettings add **Run: Hold / Toggle** and **Watch: Hold / Toggle**.\n\n### 4.3 Prompts and glyphs\n- **Source of truth:** `game.input.label(action)` (the player's bindings, `lastDevice`, `glyphs`). It returns, for example, `'✕' '○' '□' '△' 'L1' 'R2' 'Create' 'Options' 'L3' 'R3'` (PlayStation), `'A' 'B' 'X' 'Y' 'LB' 'RT' 'View' 'Menu' 'LS' 'RS'` (Xbox), and `'E' 'Shift' 'Tab' 'Space' 'Left click' 'Right click'` (keyboard/mouse).\n- **Canvas glyphs** (`Glyph.draw(g, label, x, y)` in 02-input.js), 9×9 pixel art:\n  - PlayStation: disc with symbol. ✕ `#7aa7ff` · ○ `#ff6b6b` · □ `#f08fd8` · △ `#45d6a0`.\n  - Xbox: disc with letter. A `#5dc24a` · B `#e8483c` · X `#3d8ef0` · Y `#f2c230`.\n  - Shoulders and triggers: a rounded pill with text (`L1`, `R2`, `LB`, `RT`). Create/Options/View/Menu: a small pill.\n  - Keys: a keycap box with `E.font` text. Mouse: a 7×11 mouse sprite with the pressed button lit.\n- **DOM glyphs** (`<span class=\"g g-ps-cross\">✕</span>`, `<kbd>E</kbd>`, `<span class=\"g g-mouse-l\"></span>`) use the same colors in CSS. Style E.Shell's key chips (`.esh-k`, `.esh-pr`, `.esh-keys`) with an override stylesheet so its prompts show the same colored glyphs. Don't hide the `esh-` CSS. Map label text to classes.\n- **Follow the last device.** Every frame, compare `input.lastDevice` (plus `padStyle()` and the glyph setting) with last frame's. On change, re-render DOM legends and prompts. Add hysteresis: ignore mouse moves under 6 px so a nudged mouse doesn't flip a pad player's prompts.\n- **Defaults:** PlayStation glyphs. Settings → *Button symbols: PlayStation / Xbox*. Override the standard `glyphs` setting by defining it again in `GAME_SETTINGS` with `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Game', label: 'Button symbols', options: [['playstation','PlayStation  ✕ ○ □ △'],['xbox','Xbox  A B X Y']], value: 'playstation', apply: (g, v) => g.players.forEach(p => p.glyphs = v) }`. Verify it now appears on the Settings screen. If redefining a standard key misbehaves, use a new key `padSymbols` that also calls `game.settings.set('glyphs', v)`.\n\n### 4.4 Gamepad axis inversion (the ~10% inverted-input issue)\nSettings (group *Game*): **Invert stick left/right** (`stickInvX`) and **Invert stick up/down** (`stickInvY`). Don't reuse the key `invertY`: the engine already has that one, and it only inverts the aim stick. They flip both sticks' axes for every pad and every consumer (gameplay, engine menus, E.Shell, versus) through one wrapper installed before the first `input.tick`:\n```js\nfunction installGamepadInversion(game) {\n  const real = navigator.getGamepads ? navigator.getGamepads.bind(navigator) : () => [];\n  const wrap = p => { if (!p) return p; const ix = game.settings.stickInvX, iy = game.settings.stickInvY; if (!ix && !iy) return p;\n    const a = Array.from(p.axes); if (ix) { a[0] = -a[0]; if (a.length > 2) a[2] = -a[2]; } if (iy) { a[1] = -a[1]; if (a.length > 3) a[3] = -a[3]; }\n    return { id: p.id, index: p.index, connected: p.connected, mapping: p.mapping, timestamp: p.timestamp, buttons: p.buttons, axes: a,\n             vibrationActuator: p.vibrationActuator, hapticActuators: p.hapticActuators }; };\n  try { Object.defineProperty(navigator, 'getGamepads', { configurable: true, value: () => Array.from(real() || []).map(wrap) }); } catch (e) { game.fail('gamepad inversion', e); }\n}\n```\nHelp text: \"Flips the sticks for controllers that report them backwards.\" Test it with a fake gamepad (§16).\n\n### 4.5 10-foot UI rules\n- **Type.** HTML body `font-size: clamp(18px, 2.6vh, 34px)` (about 28 px at 1080p), headings 1.6-2.4×. Canvas text uses the engine's 5×7 font at `res 'ps1'` (about 28 px glyphs at 1080p) for dialogue and barks. Never below that.\n- **Density.** At most 6 focusable items in a list, at most 8 cards in a grid page, no tables of data, one primary action per screen. Long content pages with L1/R1.\n- **Thumbnail test.** Scale a 1920×1080 screenshot to 480×270. If any essential text is unreadable there, it's too small or dense.\n- **Safe area.** 5% margins on all screen-anchored UI (TV overscan). Keep the top-right 110×60 CSS px clear for the engine's corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`).\n- **Focus.** 4 px amber `#ffb347` outline, 1.04 scale and a soft tick sound. Spatial navigation: nearest focusable in the pressed direction, by rect centers with an angle penalty. **Hover focuses** (pointerover) and click activates.\n- **Hover patch.** Add one global handler so hover moves focus in the engine's own E.Shell screens too: `document.addEventListener('pointerover', e => { if (e.pointerType !== 'mouse') return; const b = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('.esh-sh button, .esh-sh [tabindex], .esh-sh select, .esh-sh input'); if (b) b.focus({ preventScroll: true }); });` E.Shell navigates by `document.activeElement`, so this keeps them in sync.\n- **Legend.** Every screen has a footer legend built from actions (`UI.legend([['confirm','Select'],['cancel','Back'],['tabNext','Next page']])`) that re-renders on device change.\n- **Same game on every device.** Keyboard+mouse and pad use the same screens and layouts. Only prompts differ. No desktop-only layout.\n- **Color is never the only signal.** Suspicion uses icons (`·` `?` `!`) plus meters. Pertinent/non-pertinent uses icons plus text.\n\n### 4.6 HUD (E.Shell `ui.hud` each step; field scenes)\n- `objective: { step: 'EP1 · 2/4', title, text, at, from: dana }`. It shows distance and above/below (the Shell was built with `meters: .0625`, so 16 units = 1 m).\n- `status`: `Unnoticed` / `Noticed` / `Watched` / `MADE` / `Blending` / `In the van` / `Winded`, with a color *and* a word.\n- `bars`: **Heat** (max suspicion toward the player 0..1, label \"Eyes on you\"), **Stamina** (chase/tail), **Distance** (tail, §5.5).\n- `counts`: `[['Film', 27], ['Fund', '$180']]`.\n- `prompt: { act: 'interact', label: 'Talk', hint: 'Pockets' }` for the nearest/hovered interactable (§5.1).\n- `minimap: { map, at: dana, markers }` (objective, known payphones, last-seen target).\n- `ui.toast(title, text)` for photos (\"Terrence 'Tee' Mabry ★★☆ ACT\"), numbers, evidence and meter changes. `ui.tutorial(title, text)` for first-time tips (once each, stored in STATE.tips).\n- Over each watcher's head in the world: `·` (calm, only with the assist on), `?` (noticed, amber), `!` (made, red), with a 12-px suspicion bar. Same as the stealth kit's overlay.\n\n### 4.7 Settings (game group; the engine adds Display/Sound/Comfort/Controls)\n| Key | Label | Values (default first) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `glyphs` | Button symbols | PlayStation, Xbox |\n| `stickInvX` / `stickInvY` | Invert stick left/right · up/down | off |\n| `difficulty` | Case difficulty | Detective, Rookie, Murder Police |\n| `runMode` / `watchMode` | Run · Watch | Hold, Toggle |\n| `textSpeed` | Text speed | Normal (45 cps), Slow (28), Fast (80), Instant |\n| `voices` | Voice volume | slider .8 (multiplies the voice bus) |\n| `language` | Strong language | Uncensored, Bleeped (replaces a small word list with `—`) |\n| `hints` | Investigation hints | on: highlights key wire lines on Rookie, Szabo hints after 2 failed tries |\n| `cones` | Show sight cones | on (assist; `r.sightCone` for watchers when you're near) |\n| `edgePeek` | Mouse edge peek | on |\n\nDifficulty multiplies suspicion rise (Rookie .7 · Detective 1 · Murder Police 1.35), the minimization window (10 s · 8 s · 6 s), Brass decay (.7 · 1 · 1.3) and tail tolerance (lost after 16 s · 12 s · 9 s).\n\n### 4.8 Touch-only devices\n- **Detection** at boot and on every input: `touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`, and no gamepad connected, and no keydown seen yet.\n- **What shows:** the title shows a centered panel, \"**Controller or keyboard required**\" (subtext: \"Connect a controller or keyboard to play. The demo will run in the meantime.\"), and a large **Settings** button. Tap it → `game.menu('settings')`.\n- **No touch controls anywhere:** never pass `touch:` in scenes, set `game.input.touchEnabled = false`, and `game.settings.set('touch', 'off')` on touch-only devices.\n- **Attract still runs:** after 10 s it starts, and a tap returns to the panel.\n- **Recovery:** `gamepadconnected` or any `keydown` hides the panel and shows the normal menu.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Core systems\n\n### 5.0 Shared numbers\n| Thing | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Tile | 16 units; map 72×56 cells = 1152×896 units |","numLines":320,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1822}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01E5pAEHbWXiW4JD3DFyXY6U","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
