{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Htw1LAvjqT55H8pk5oNPcB","type":"tool_result","content":"205\t  - fists: `jab` (left hand) `cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow`;\n206\t  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n207\t  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n208\t  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n209\t- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n210\t- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n211\t- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n212\t- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (victory with the weapon raised, spell casting, a boxer's guard, hurt or resting, ducking, greeting, a cocky idle, knocked out; they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon held upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, the knees give, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat (centred on its position). `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n213\t- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n214\t**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n215\t\n216\t## Effects and lighting\n217\t- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n218\t- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n219\t- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n220\t- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n221\t\n222\t## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n223\t| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n224\t|---|---|---|---|\n225\t| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n226\t| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n227\t| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n228\t| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n229\t| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n230\t| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n231\t| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n232\t| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n233\t| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n234\t| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n235\t| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n236\t| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n237\t| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n238\t| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n239\t| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n240\t| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n241\t| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n242\t| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n243\t| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n244\t| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n245\t| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n246\t| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n247\t| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n248\t| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n249\t| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n250\t| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n251\t| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n252\t| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n253\t| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n254\t| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n255\t| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n256\t| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n257\t\n258\t**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n259\t\n260\t**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n261\t\n262\t## More: animation and examples outside this card\n263\tThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs.\n264\t- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n265\t- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n266\t- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes (one a living book, one a scythed beast) and 30 skills, registered with `def('archetypes', ...)`, `def('characters', ...)` and `def('skills', ...)`, so one search lists them. Adapt them; they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n267\t- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n268\t  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges (over heights, past allies), lines, fields of view (symmetric shadowcasting), `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, facing, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n269\t  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n270\t  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (a Platformer and a Humanoid; frame data; hurt boxes from the rig's joints; high, low and overhead guards; chip; throws; cancels; juggles; knockdowns; specials by motion or charge; a super meter; projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, timer, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n271\t  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges (a slide or a fade, the camera's bounds), the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n272\t  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera at a height over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a color function's or a canvas), drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with the heading (a Backdrop), billboards far to near (rigs at the size their distance gives, sprites, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, a split screen's cameras. The stress test's depth view, KART.\n273\t  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves (OutRun, Pole Position). ROAD.\n274\t  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: speed, grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, the floor's tag slowing it, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, wrong way, places, drivers that brake for bends, pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` races too). KART.\n275\t  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): nodes of lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values (`{$name}` in lines), gotos, the game's events (one may hold the talk), a quest log, emote balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n276\t  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n277\t  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): real-time strategy and sims (`E.Strategy`): selection (a click, a box, a double tap, groups), orders (move, attack, attack-move, gather, build, patrol; queued), formations on shared flow fields kept apart, production, resources, placement with a ghost, fog, the minimap, an enemy's build order and waves; `E.Clock` (days, night, the light), needs that pick an action (`E.needs`, `E.choose`), `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n278\t  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): rooms painted once, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`: the shortest way round), depth scale, hotspots (rectangles, polygons, actors) with verbs, a place to stand and a way to face, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory (an item on a hotspot or another item), rules as data, exits with fades, `story`'s flags and talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n279\t  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, convex polygons, static segments, chains and arcs; dynamic, kinematic and static bodies; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep, gravity as a vector; fast balls swept so they never pass through; stepped in a fixed order (the same world at any refresh rate); flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY (its grenades).\n280\t  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): a mask of materials carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, hits and rays for shots, walkers on slopes (Worms, Lemmings), a collider for `physics`, drawn in chunks redrawn only where it changed. ARTILLERY.\n281\t  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): the kits' best songs and more by mood (field, town, shop, battle, race, puzzle, space, horror, stealth, chase, dance), and jingles (stage clear, game over, one-up, level up, fanfare); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n282\t  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps (lane strings, parts in an order, or a track's notes), judgments in ms of the music clock (perfect, great, good, miss), combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n283\t  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n284\t- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n285\t\n286\t## Before you hand the game back\n287\t- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n288\t- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n289\t- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n290\t- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n291\t- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n292\t- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n293\t- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n294\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5584c513-9280-4b46-aaca-c597f867a104","uuid":"35082ff4-fa23-4f61-a8d3-c5c133e6da9d","timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:59:24.255Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/s55/wt-engine/dist/handoff/API.md","content":"  - fists: `jab` (left hand) `cross hook uppercut haymaker elbow`;\n  - kicks: `kick roundhouse sweep flyingkick knee axekick`;\n  - other: `cast` (both hands) `throw bash claw`.\n  Give each character its own set (a knight: `overhead`, `thrust`, `spin`; a mage: `cast`; a monk: `jab`, `cross`, `roundhouse`; a boss: `haymaker`, `sweep`). `E.move('uppercut', .5)` is the attack state of any move at any moment (previews, cutscenes).\n- `attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }`, spec `{ a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, r0, r1, hand, rel, blade, spin, kick, plane, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`. Angles are relative to facing (+ = right hand side). `rel: true`: heights relative to the shoulder and reach scaled by arm length (kick heights as a share of hip height). `r0`/`r1`: reach that extends (thrusts, jabs). `hand: 'L' | 'both'`. In side views horizontal swings turn into the screen plane (a Castlevania chop) by themselves; `plane: 'side' | 'ground'` (or the rig option `swingPlane`) chooses. `lunge` steps into the strike (body motion in local units, clamped to -4..5; to move the character itself, add speed to your body), `hop` lifts the body, `crouch` dips in the wind-up, `hold` is how long the end pose holds (share of recover). You time the phases.\n- In steep ground views (threequarter) rigs and Blobs are drawn from a lower, sprite-like angle so faces show, like SNES RPG sprites (`charView: false` turns it off for one rig, `E.style.charPitch = false` for all).\n- Faces (eyes with catch-lights, brows, mouth), a cheated 3/4 turn toward the camera in side views (`cheat: .5`, 0 turns it off) and hair layers are automatic. `face: { eyes: 'big' (anime / Mana style), bangs: 0..1 }`.\n- Poses: `rig.update(dt, { ..., pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' })` (victory with the weapon raised, spell casting, a boxer's guard, hurt or resting, ducking, greeting, a cocky idle, knocked out; they blend in and out), `'block'` (weapon held upright across the body), `'die'` (a stagger, the knees give, then the body topples), `expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince'` (faces and portraits), `run: 0..1` (run in place), or `down: 0..1` to lie a defeated body flat (centred on its position). `stance: 'guard'` keeps fists up while walking (brawlers; the free hand guards the chin during punches), `stance: 'ready'` holds the weapon forward. `climb: true` with `vz` climbs a ladder hand over hand (`Platformer.rigState()` passes both). Traversal: `crouch` (a sneaking gait), `sprint` (a lean), `hang` with `hangZ` (hands on an edge that high above the feet), `flip: 0..1` (a roll), `land: k` (a heavy landing); `mover.rigState()` sets them. Every wind-up starts from the rig's current pose, so chained hits and hand changes never snap. Idle rigs breathe and shift their weight by themselves.\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)` inside `r.actor`, `rig.drawSmear(r)` (a glowing trail along the real blade, fist or foot path, in every view), `rig.debug(r)` (skeleton), `rig.kick(v)` (squash and stretch), `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()` (world points to spawn bullets from), `rig.worldOffset(p)` (where a point in the rig's own frame, such as a joint in `rig.J`, sits from its feet: to draw or spawn at any joint), `rig.lastView` (the view it last drew with), `rig.solvePose()` (sets `rig.J` from its state each step: wrap it on one rig to move joints).\n**Blob** (slimes, rabbites, bats, imps, round monsters): `new E.Blob({ R: 6, colors: { dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil }, ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true, feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' })`, `update(dt, { squash: -0.4..0.4, look: [dx, dy], squint, walk: 0..1, flap: 0..1 (wing speed; 0 folds them), hang: true (roost upside down) })`, `kick(v)`, `draw(g, ox, oy, view)`. Mix the parts for a bestiary: `wings` + `mouth: 'fangs'` is a bat, `ears: 'rabbit'` + `feet` a Mana rabbite, `horns` + `tail` an imp.\n\n## Effects and lighting\n- Particles: `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size)` (fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, ring, shake, sound), `fire(x, y, z, n, { size })`, `smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color })`, `glints(x, y, z, n, color)`, `dust(x, y, z, n)`, `sparks(x, y, z, n, angle)`, `bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`, `ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur)`, `text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true })` (bouncing damage numbers), `add({ kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'fire' | 'smoke' | 'glint', ... })`. `explosion(x, y, z, size, { debris, freeze: false, shake: 0 })` for chains of small blasts. Side-scrollers: `particles.ground = (x, y, z) => level.groundBelow(x, z)` so sparks and debris bounce on platforms. Screen flash: `game.flash('#ffffff', .12, strength 0..1)`.\n- Lighting (step 9; every view, side-scrollers included, no WebGPU needed): `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color: '#ffa050' })` every draw. Dark areas get a smooth cool tint, lights add their own color, and emissive items (flames, sparks, bullets, `emissive: true`) stay bright. `lights.darkness` (0..1) and `lights.tint` tune it; after setting `lights.dark = [r, g, b]`, `levels` or `glow`, call `lights.refresh()`. Props with flames can light the scene: `r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { light: true })`. Lamps, street lamps, lanterns and crystals glow on their own (`haloR`, `haloColor`, `halo: false`); `cone: true` adds a light cone under a street lamp.\n- Hits: `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage: 12, textScale: 2, textZ: 22 })` does hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks, a bouncing damage number (`bounce: false` floats it) and a sound in one call; `particles.impact(x, y, z, size, color)` for the star alone.\n- GPU lighting (optional, ground views with a TileMap): `const gpu = game.enableGPU({ map })`; mark glowing things with `px.glow(g, 1)`; add `lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` and `lights.heat(x, y, z, w)`. Where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `game.lights.ambient` (`gpu.fallback = false`: unlit).\n\n## Genre playbook (NES, SNES, Genesis, N64, PS1)\n| Genre (examples) | View | Build it with | Copy slice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Platformer (Mario, Mega Man, Sonic, Castlevania) | `side` (+ `brawler` for 2.5D) | PlatformMap, Platformer, Humanoid, sprites, Bullets `'side'` | PLATFORMER |\n| Metroidvania (Metroid, Symphony of the Night) | `side` | an `E.World` of PlatformMaps (the rooms module: doors at open edges, a slide, the explored map, gates opened by abilities, saves), a Platformer given `airJumps` and `dash` as they are found, its further moves, an arm cannon aimed eight ways (`point`, `aim`, `rig.tip()`, Bullets) | METROIDVANIA |\n| Action adventure (Zelda, Secret of Mana) | `threequarter` or `topdown` | TileMap rows, `cam.room`, Body, Humanoid + `E.Attack` sword, Dialog, `E.ui.hearts` | ADVENTURE |\n| Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword) | none: rooms painted in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) | `E.Click` (the click module): rooms with walk areas and holes (`E.Walkbox`), depth scale, hotspots, the nine verbs, Sierra's icons or a verb coin, an inventory, puzzles as rules; talk through `E.Story` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad) | none | `E.Story` nodes and choices over a painted backdrop, `rig.drawPortrait` at size, `story.log` (the backlog), `story.auto`, `story.skip`; `game.saves` | POINT-AND-CLICK |\n| Action RPG / dungeon (Diablo, Landstalker) | `iso` | TileMap, FlowField, Body (jump onto blocks), GPU lighting | ADVENTURE |\n| Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage, TMNT) | `brawler` | TileMap street strip, Body, Humanoid (`weapon: null`) + `E.Combo` punches and `kick: true` kicks, `E.knockback`, waves that lock the screen; a second player drops in (`game.allowJoin`, `cam.frame` on both) | BRAWLER |\n| Fighting (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers) | `side` | two `E.Fighter`s in an `E.Match` (the fight module): moves with frame data, guards, throws, specials by motion (`'236'`) or charge, a meter, projectiles; `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training | FIGHTING |\n| Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius, R-Type) | `overhead` (vertical, and horizontal: world x scrolls) | sprites, Bullets, `E.pattern`, a timeline of waves; horizontal: rock edges as functions of x, a power bar, options on the ship's past path, `E.Path` loops; `E.Arcade` for the continue and the table | SHOOTER (both stages) |\n| Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet, Devil's Crush) | a steep table view of its own (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`) | `E.Physics`: chains for the walls, `flipper`, `bumper`, `slingshot`, `kicker`, a ramp on a layer of its own, the ball a `bullet`; `input.zones` flip on a phone; `E.Arcade` for the score and table. Breakout and Asteroids with real collisions are its recipes | PINBALL |\n| Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth) | `side` at a whole scale (`new E.View(id, label, 0, 0, 1, 1)`) | `E.Terrain` (`carve` the blasts, `walk` the soldiers, `ray` the shots), grenades as `E.Physics` bodies on `land.collider()`, `E.Turns`; a drag aims (`steer = 'drag'`, `drag()`, `stickOn`); a CPU tries its shots in simulation | ARTILLERY |\n| Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids) | `overhead` (one fixed screen, cabinet art on the sides) | `E.Arcade` (score, lives, extra lives, turns, initials, tables, attract mode); a maze of `E.GridMover`s; moths on `E.Path`s turned by `rot`; `E.wrap` and `wrap: [w, h]` | ARCADE |\n| Twin-stick / run-and-gun (Smash TV, Contra) | `topdown` / `side` | Body or Platformer, Bullets, `weapon: 'gun'`, `point: true` | SHOOTER + ADVENTURE |\n| JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy, EarthBound) | `threequarter` town, `brawler` battles | TileMap towns and a big world map, the story and rpg modules, Dialog with markup, `game.saves` slots | RPG |\n| Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo, Columns) | `side` (a field in screen pixels) | a cell array, a bag from `E.rng`, `input.repeat` (auto-shift), `grid.groups` (chains), `input.drag()` and `clicked()` for touch; versus: two fields, rows sent across (`game.players`) | PUZZLE |\n| Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren) | `threequarter` / `iso` | `E.gen` floors, a TileMap of its rows, `E.Grid.from(map)` paths and `fov`, `E.Fog`, `E.Turns` by speed, `game.tween` steps | ROGUELIKE |\n| Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem) | `iso`, turned a quarter | TileMap of block heights, `map.pick` under the cursor, `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true })` reach over heights, `grid.shape`, `grid.side`, `grid.threat`, `E.Turns` charge time, `E.Story` | TACTICS |\n| Real-time strategy (Warcraft II, StarCraft, Dune II) | `iso` | `E.Strategy` (the strategy module): kinds as data, selection by click, box and group, orders, gold, building, training, fog, an AI's build order and waves; `cam.free`, `{ pick: unit }` and `r.pick`, buildings as `E.SHAPES`, E.Shell's minimap; bodies drawn `every: 3` | RTS |\n| Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity) | `iso` | paths as floor tags walked on an `E.Grid`, agents with `E.needs` picking by `E.choose`, `E.Clock` days and light, `game.timeScale` 0 to 4, `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`; rides as shapes stepped through angles (`params`, `facing`) | SIM |\n| 3D platformer or adventure remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash) | `iso` / `threequarter` / `brawler` | TileMap blocks of different heights + Body `jump()` (z is real height); in real 3D: `E.World3D` (the 3D engine's manual), `camera.lock` | ADVENTURE; 3D ACTION, 3D PLATFORMER |\n| Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark) | the 3D engine's `'zones'` camera | `camera.zones` (fixed cameras by room), `camera.steer` (tank controls), `camera.lock` to aim, `world.through` doors, `world.flashlight`, fog; `E.RPG` inventory, `game.saves` | 3D HORROR |\n| First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder) | the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes | cells of 2 x 2 tiles stepped and turned a quarter at a time (tweened), `E.Grid` paths for the monsters, `E.Turns` `'atb'` (real time) or `'speed'` (turns), `E.Fog` for the automap, `world.thing('door')` gates and `world.unlock`, `rig.drawPortrait` for the party | 3D CRAWLER |\n| Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania, Space Channel 5) | any (a highway on the overlay) | `E.Rhythm` (the rhythm module): a chart of lane strings in parts on a song's steps, judged in ms of the music clock, combo, life, grade, holds, calls a teacher plays and you answer, `rh.draw` (`arrows: true`); the songbook's songs (`E.Songbook`); lanes as `input.zones` on a phone; `audio.onBeat` moves the dancer | RHYTHM |\n| Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam) | `threequarter` / `topdown` | the ball an `E.Body` (`bounce`; curl: turn its velocity across its path), passes led to the teammate, `E.arc` for a lob, a shot's meter, formations by where the ball is, `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` | SPORTS |\n| Third-person stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu) | `iso` / `threequarter` | TileMap roofs, Mover, `E.sees`, `E.Suspicion`, FlowField `jump` | STEALTH |\n| Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero) | Mode 7 (the mode7 module, over any view) | `E.Mode7` over a TileMap track (floor tags: grass, sand, boost), `E.Car` karts (`kart: true`: hop, drift, boost) in an `E.Race` round an `E.Path` (laps, checkpoints, drivers, items), rigs in karts as billboards, `game.splitScreen(2)` for two | KART |\n| 3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.track` (banked, walls, pads, `gaps` for a shortcut), `world.vehicle` karts raced by `E.Race`, split screen through `world.cameras` | 3D RACING |\n| Rail shooters (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon) | the 3D engine's `'behind'` camera | `world.flyer` on rails or free in bounds, rolls (`f.rolling`) turn shots aside, `f.seek` for a charged lock, `Dialog`'s radio | 3D FLIGHT |\n| Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Road Rash) | the pseudo-3D road (the road module) | `E.Road` segments (curves, hills, tunnels), scenery and traffic placed on it, `curveAt` throwing the car outward, a clock and checkpoints | ROAD |\n| Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA) | `topdown` / `iso` | `E.Car` on a TileMap (its floor tags slow it, its walls bounce it), `E.Race` for laps and drivers, cars drawn with `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points | KART |\n| First-person shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007, Apex Legends) | the 3D engine's first person (or third) | a level of text rows or a terrain, `world.ray` hitscan, recoil and reloads, doors (`world.thing('door')`), raiders on `world.flow()` | 3D SHOOTER |\n| Bomberman, Frogger, Sokoban, Gauntlet, Robotron; Sonic, Prince of Persia; light guns | as the recipe says | `AI_GUIDE.md`'s Genre recipes: each on the kit closest to it | (a recipe) |\n\n**Sizes:** heroes 40-60 px tall at 320x240 (`size: 1.2-1.4`), bosses 1.8-2.4. Small things (coins, bullets, ships, pickups) are string sprites designed at 8-16 px, drawn at scale 2 if they are the player's ship. Keep hitboxes smaller than the art.\n\n**Feel numbers** (world units per second, tile = 16): walk 70-90, run 95-140, jump 250-320 with gravity 800, fall cap 330, dash 200-260, bullets 250-320 (player) and 70-120 (enemy), enemy walk 25-45. Hit feedback: `game.hitFx` (Effects).\n\n## More: animation and examples outside this card\nThe repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge; any file at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`) holds more to take from. Its files are large: search them and take only what the game needs.\n- **Motion clips**: 325 curated and 2,548 motion-capture takes that play on any Humanoid: find one in `src/mocap/catalogs/`, cut a small set with `tools/anim-set.mjs`, then `Mocap.drive(rig, lib)` (`AI_GUIDE.md`, Where to find more; `docs/MOCAP.md`).\n- **Objects and materials** (full engine): `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a still object from any view and facing, `map.solid(...)` makes it solid; `E.MATERIALS` dresses floors (`floorMat`), walls (`face`, `roof`) and platform tiles (`mat`). `docs/ASSETS.md` is the card: the format, the library, writing one and checking it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`.\n- **Worked examples**: Emberdeep (`src/emberdeep/`), a whole game on this engine: 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies, 3 heroes (one a living book, one a scythed beast) and 30 skills, registered with `def('archetypes', ...)`, `def('characters', ...)` and `def('skills', ...)`, so one search lists them. Adapt them; they lean on the game's own helpers. `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body.\n- **Modules** (`engine/modules/<name>.js`; `modules/` in the handoff package): general systems in files of their own, each a `<script>` after the engine, its header its manual (read it there, or its section of the index). A kit carries the modules its slice names (`@modules` in its header); the full engine (`dist/my-3d2dge.js`; `my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) carries them all.\n  - `grid`: square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges (over heights, past allies), lines, fields of view (symmetric shadowcasting), `E.Fog`, groups, shapes of effect, facing, threat maps, `E.GridMover`, `E.Turns` (speed, charge time, phases, ATB). ROGUELIKE, PUZZLE, TACTICS, ARCADE, ARTILLERY, RTS, SIM, 3D CRAWLER.\n  - `gen`: generated maps from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a TileMap reads. ROGUELIKE, ARCADE.\n  - `fight` (the full engine only): one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter` (a Platformer and a Humanoid; frame data; hurt boxes from the rig's joints; high, low and overhead guards; chip; throws; cancels; juggles; knockdowns; specials by motion or charge; a super meter; projectiles), `E.Match` (rounds, timer, KO, the HUD, training), `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`. FIGHTING.\n  - `rooms`: a world of rooms on a grid of screens (`E.World`): walking between them at open edges (a slide or a fade, the camera's bounds), the explored map (`drawMap`, or `shellMap()` for `E.Shell`), gates that abilities or keys open, saves in `E.store`. METROIDVANIA.\n  - `mode7`: the SNES's Mode 7 (`E.Mode7`): a camera at a height over a flat floor (a TileMap's, a color function's or a canvas), drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with the heading (a Backdrop), billboards far to near (rigs at the size their distance gives, sprites, your drawing), fog, `project` and `toFloor`, a split screen's cameras. The stress test's depth view, KART.\n  - `road`: the pseudo-3D road (`E.Road`): segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance and hidden past a crest, the sky's parallax on curves (OutRun, Pole Position). ROAD.\n  - `drive`: cars on the ground plane (`E.Car`: speed, grip and slide, a kart's hop and drift boost, the floor's tag slowing it, walls and cars it bounces off) and races (`E.Race`: checkpoints along an `E.Path`, laps, wrong way, places, drivers that brake for bends, pass and rubber-band, items, the HUD; the 3D engine's `world.vehicle` races too). KART.\n  - `story`: dialogue as data (`E.Story`): nodes of lines with speakers and portraits, choices with conditions, flags and values (`{$name}` in lines), gotos, the game's events (one may hold the talk), a quest log, emote balloons over heads, a backlog, auto and skip; its state for a save. RPG, POINT-AND-CLICK, TACTICS.\n  - `rpg`: a role-playing game's rules (`E.RPG`): items, bags (slots or a grid of sizes), members with stats, levels and gear, status effects, a party (items used, rewards shared), shops, damage, random encounters by zone, list and card windows. RPG, 3D HORROR.\n  - `strategy` (the full engine only; needs `grid`): real-time strategy and sims (`E.Strategy`): selection (a click, a box, a double tap, groups), orders (move, attack, attack-move, gather, build, patrol; queued), formations on shared flow fields kept apart, production, resources, placement with a ghost, fog, the minimap, an enemy's build order and waves; `E.Clock` (days, night, the light), needs that pick an action (`E.needs`, `E.choose`), `E.overlay`, `E.tooltip`. RTS, SIM.\n  - `click` (the full engine only): point-and-click adventures (`E.Click`): rooms painted once, walk areas with holes (`E.Walkbox`: the shortest way round), depth scale, hotspots (rectangles, polygons, actors) with verbs, a place to stand and a way to face, SCUMM's nine verbs, Sierra's icons, a verb coin or two buttons, an inventory (an item on a hotspot or another item), rules as data, exits with fades, `story`'s flags and talk; taps on a phone. POINT-AND-CLICK.\n  - `physics`: 2D rigid bodies (`E.Physics`): circles, boxes, convex polygons, static segments, chains and arcs; dynamic, kinematic and static bodies; bounce, friction, sensors, layers, sleep, gravity as a vector; fast balls swept so they never pass through; stepped in a fixed order (the same world at any refresh rate); flippers, bumpers, slingshots, kickers. PINBALL, ARTILLERY (its grenades).\n  - `terrain`: destructible ground in the side plane (`E.Terrain`): a mask of materials carved and added by circles and polygons, the ground below a point and its normal, hits and rays for shots, walkers on slopes (Worms, Lemmings), a collider for `physics`, drawn in chunks redrawn only where it changed. ARTILLERY.\n  - `songs`: the songbook (`E.Songbook`): the kits' best songs and more by mood (field, town, shop, battle, race, puzzle, space, horror, stealth, chase, dance), and jingles (stage clear, game over, one-up, level up, fanfare); `get(id)`, `find(mood)`. RHYTHM.\n  - `rhythm` (the full engine only): `E.Rhythm`: charts on a song's steps (lane strings, parts in an order, or a track's notes), judgments in ms of the music clock (perfect, great, good, miss), combo, score, a life gauge, grades, holds, call and response, a note highway in any direction. RHYTHM.\n  - `arcade`: the coin-op's frame: scores that float up, extra lives, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode (a demo, the table, the title). ARCADE, SHOOTER, PINBALL.\n- **Everything running**: https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs\n\n## Before you hand the game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge` (between the markers in a single file; `game/game.js` in the handoff package). Only your game: no leftover starter menu or unused slices. No network, no libraries.\n- Title screen and game-over screen as scenes; `pausable: true` on gameplay scenes. No pause, settings, rebinding or full-screen code of your own (they're standard); prompts from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player (`game.focus` every update) and shows the level; the HUD uses screen pixels, or `E.Shell`'s HTML for a modern game (the STEALTH kit; the handoff package's template).\n- Sound effects on jump, hit, pickup and death; music per scene.\n- The look target list above: backdrop, textured tiles, props, sized and dressed HD characters, effects, title font, lights. Compare your screenshots with Symphony of the Night or Metal Slug, not with NES games.\n- If you can run commands: `node tools/check.mjs game/index.html` (your page; add `#scene` to jump to a scene). It presses start, plays, cycles the views, prints how much of the screen is filled and how many colors it uses, adds look notes (flat areas, checkerboard dithering, low contrast, a thin palette), and fails on any error. Act on every look note. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html#scene --steps \"wait:1500 press:Enter wait:800 rec:16:2 press:KeyJ\" --crop x,y,w,h` records an animation frame by frame into one contact sheet: check every attack with it (a swing must wind up, strike, and settle back without jerks or backward sweeps).\n- Animation variety: every character has its own moves from `E.MOVES` (not one shared swing), enemies telegraph with a visible wind-up, and reactions play out (hurt, knockback, `down`, victory `cheer`).\n","numLines":90,"startLine":205,"totalLines":294}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Htw1LAvjqT55H8pk5oNPcB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cggb2f3TDtxf4YvGvTA4e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013WaiYcVZi4NWX3Q5gLmtYE","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-125821__x600c07/work; sed -n 838,900p PLAN.md; sed -n 1081,1133p PLAN.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":26401,"cache_read_input_tokens":42703,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":26401,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5584c513-9280-4b46-aaca-c597f867a104","uuid":"4750fe02-29ac-43eb-a24d-42091fe5f1fc","timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:59:25.043Z","request_id":"req_011Cggb2egP24YpKyGEWLfJm","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_013WaiYcVZi4NWX3Q5gLmtYE":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261103-125821__x600c07/work; sed -n 838,900p PLAN.md; sed -n 1081,1133p PLAN.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013WaiYcVZi4NWX3Q5gLmtYE","type":"tool_result","content":"### 9.3 AI Hub: shared-world (AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n**Tags** (in `<head>`, after the client script):\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Cops: build a 100-point case. Corner: make $1,200 unseen.\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: stick/WASD · Watch: L2/RMB · Photo: R2/LMB · Act: ✕/E · Call out: △/Q\">\n```\n**Flow** (99-start.js):\n```js\nif (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) (async () => {         // async wrapper: the game's IIFE itself is not async\n  game.start({ scenes, scene: 'hubWait', boot });        // 'Connecting…' (no prompts): straight into the round, no title/attract/menus\n  AIHub.on('start', () => Versus.countdown());           // overlay: role card + 3-2-1 until AIHub.time() >= 0\n  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4] });   // the hub's own card (no card: true)\n  if (!info) { game.go('title'); return; }               // no hub after all: the normal game\n  await Versus.buildRound(info);                         // roster from AIHub.rng('roster'); sides from seats + info.round\n  AIHub.ready();                                         // once, with no key press\n})().catch(e => { game.fail('hub', e); AIHub.fail(String((e && e.message) || e)); });\nelse game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot });\n```\n- **Host authority** (`info.isHost`): the host copy runs `VersusSim`: NPCs, crew AI, bots, rules, scoring, the timer (from `AIHub.time()`).\n- **Every copy moves its own character locally** (no input lag) and reports it. The host trusts positions inside a speed check.\n- **Other copies render snapshots** with 150 ms interpolation.\n- **Messages:**\n  - Host → all, unreliable, 10 Hz: `{t:'s', at, tl, ev, cn, ph, p:[[id,x,y,f,st,fl],…], n:[[nid,x,y,f,st],…]}`.\n    - x, y are integers (world units); f is facing quantized 0-63; st is an animation code (0 idle, 1 walk, 2 run, 3 crouch, 4 watch, 5 act, 6 down, 7 sit, 8 phone, 9 bike); fl is flags (burned, carrying, shut).\n    - **Keep each message under 1,100 bytes.** If the NPC list makes it bigger, send NPCs in two alternating halves (5 Hz each).\n  - Host → all, reliable, on events: `{t:'e', k:'photo'|'burn'|'callout'|'tap'|'jump'|'seize'|'stash'|'reup'|'shut'|'open'|'sale', …}` (sounds, toasts, particles).\n  - Client → host, unreliable, 10 Hz: `{t:'me', at, x, y, f, st, w, ax, ay}` (w = watching; ax, ay = the aimed world point).\n  - Client → host, reliable: `{t:'act', k:'photo', target, q, at}` · `{k:'callout', target}` · `{k:'tap', phone}` · `{k:'jump', target}` · `{k:'shut'|'open'|'stash'|'reup'|'pickup'|'handoff'|'ditch', …}`.\n  - Budget: host 10 snapshots + under 5 events per second (< 20/s); clients 10 + a few.\n- **Validation on the host:**\n  - Positions are clamped to `maxSpeed × elapsed × 1.5` and to walkable cells.\n  - A photo claim needs: the target exists, is within 240 units of the claimer, and `map.sight` is clear on the host. If ★Act is claimed, the host's 1-second act history must show an act within 600 ms.\n  - Call-outs are checked against the true identity.\n- **Events:**\n  - `leave` → that player's character becomes a bot on the same side; the round goes on.\n  - The host leaving voids the round (the hub sends `end` `host-left`) → show \"Round cancelled\".\n  - `time-up` → the host finishes at once with the timer-end outcome.\n  - `end` → stop input (controllers to null), stop music and effects, show our results under the hub's. After `end`, send nothing.\n- **Server** (`AIHub.server`): the host has no local player. It runs the sim and shows a spectator camera (`cam.frame` over all players). No audio, no input.\n- **Finish** (host only, once):\n  - `standings`: winners `[[winning side ids],[losing side ids]]`; draw `[[all ids]]`. Use only ids from `AIHub.players` that haven't left (left players may be omitted: the hub places them last). Never bots, never the server's own id.\n  - `won`: when only one side has humans (one player vs bots, or every human on one side), pass `won: true` or `false` for that side. Standings alone can't say it.\n  - `scores`: `{ id: personalScore }`.\n  - `stats`: `{ id: { Photos, Burns, 'Call-outs', Count } }` (numbers or short strings).\n  - `awards`: `[{ id, title, detail }]`, e.g. `{ id: 2, title: 'Eyes On', detail: '4 hand-to-hand photos' }`.\n- **Session rules:**\n  - **Round length:** `min(300 s, info.limitMs − 5 s)`, so the host always finishes before the hub's hard limit (the harness sets it with `&limit=`).\n  - **Hidden host tab:** the engine's animation loop stops in a hidden tab. While `document.hidden` on the host, step the sim from a `setInterval` that calls `game.advance(elapsed)` (real elapsed time, capped at 1 s per call; browsers may throttle it to 1 Hz) and keep sending snapshots, so the other players don't freeze. Check that `advance` behaves while the loop is suspended. The hub voids the round after 20 s hidden anyway.\n  - **Names** from the hub are text: render them with `textContent` or canvas text (`E.font`), never as HTML.\n  - No other network use.\n  - No `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`, no new windows.\n  - Pause doesn't stop the host: the versus scene is `pausable: false` in a session. Options opens a non-pausing overlay with Settings/Controls (`game.menu('settings')`) while the character's input is suppressed.\n  - `menu.quit: false` and no restart items.\n  - While Create (Pad8) is held, ignore Options.\n- **Randomness:** the host never uses `Math.random` for the sim. Shared rolls come from `AIHub.rng(label)`, and the roster from `AIHub.rng('roster')`, so every copy builds identical NPC rigs.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Attract mode (52-attract.js)\n## 13. About/Credits screen and the exit interview\n- **`credits.json` is the single source** of the About pages and of `exit-interview.json`. The build copies its `exitInterview` object verbatim into `../exit-interview.json` and verifies it parses.\n- **About & Credits** (title menu → DOM screen; L1/R1 pages; large type; at most about 10 short lines per page; stick scrolls long answers):\n  1. **Credits:**\n     - WIRETAP; \"An original game inspired by *The Wire* (HBO, 2002-2008). Not affiliated with HBO or the show's creators.\"\n     - Design and code: the model(s) that built it (truthfully: if the builder isn't claude-opus-5-5 at max effort, list both, e.g. \"Planned by Anthropic claude-opus-5-5 (max); built by <id>\").\n     - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 by Michael Crosato (MIT). Multiplayer: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0.\n     - Any CC0 asset entries used (Kenney/KayKit via the engine's store), each with its source line.\n  2. **Source Information:** every field of TASK.md's list, same text as `exitInterview.sourceInformation`.\n  3. **Testing Feedback:** the 4 questions with answers.\n  4. **Engine Feedback:** the 6 questions with answers.\n  5. **Prompt Feedback:** the 4 questions with answers.\n- **Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.** Fill in real answers at the end of the build: no placeholders ship.\n- **Exit interview shape** (exactly TASK.md's). Suggested values to start from; correct any that turn out untrue:\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-03 10:53\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth investigation / crime drama with a corner-management sim and an asymmetric versus mode\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Isometric 2.5D (pixel-art 3D skeletons on a tile world); brawler-view interiors; HTML case-file screens\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1 full engine (canvas 2D, optional WebGPU lighting); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; vanilla JavaScript; Web Audio\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"Engine-native retro-modern pixel art (PS1-era 2D look) pushed toward grit: sodium streetlight, rain, Formstone rowhouses, boarded vacants\",\n    \"animation\": \"Engine-native procedural Humanoid rigs (E.MOVES, poses, E.ACTIONS paired actions) plus custom joint post-processed poses; tweens and coroutine scripts\",\n    \"audio\": \"Code-written chip-synth music (engine music tracks, layered), synthesized effects and ambience, Web Audio formant gibberish voices with a telephone filter\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world versus for 1-4 players (host-authoritative); local split-screen versus; no other network\",\n    \"testing\": \"Lightweight, token-efficient testing as TASK.md allows (FPS targets and optimization out of scope). Run: <replace with what you actually ran, e.g. headless Node tests on the engine's fixed-step clock, engine check.mjs screenshots, Playwright gamepad/touch checks, the AI Hub harness, a viewport-matrix spot check>\",\n    \"research\": \"my-3D2dge handoff docs (API.md, AI_GUIDE.md, ASSETS.md, kits) and the AI Hub protocol README; knowledge of the series from memory; no other web research\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\",\n    \"usage\": \"unknown\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"WIRETAP\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Somebody's always listening.\",\n    \"description\": \"Work both sides of a West Baltimore corner in 2004: build a wiretap case one photo, page and phone call at a time, then run the corner the case is built on. Includes a 1-4 player cops-vs-corner versus mode.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Stick/WASD move · L2/RMB watch · R2/LMB photo · ✕/E interact · △/Q partner · Create/Tab notebook\",\n    \"tags\": [\"story\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"isometric\", \"crime\", \"detective\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n- **The build's final chat reply** must also contain Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback (TASK.md).\n\n---","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5584c513-9280-4b46-aaca-c597f867a104","uuid":"c451fcd6-855d-4e06-ba0d-346996b60326","timestamp":"2026-11-03T19:59:25.059Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### 9.3 AI Hub: shared-world (AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n**Tags** (in `<head>`, after the client script):\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Cops: build a 100-point case. Corner: make $1,200 unseen.\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move: stick/WASD · Watch: L2/RMB · Photo: R2/LMB · Act: ✕/E · Call out: △/Q\">\n```\n**Flow** (99-start.js):\n```js\nif (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) (async () => {         // async wrapper: the game's IIFE itself is not async\n  game.start({ scenes, scene: 'hubWait', boot });        // 'Connecting…' (no prompts): straight into the round, no title/attract/menus\n  AIHub.on('start', () => Versus.countdown());           // overlay: role card + 3-2-1 until AIHub.time() >= 0\n  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4] });   // the hub's own card (no card: true)\n  if (!info) { game.go('title'); return; }               // no hub after all: the normal game\n  await Versus.buildRound(info);                         // roster from AIHub.rng('roster'); sides from seats + info.round\n  AIHub.ready();                                         // once, with no key press\n})().catch(e => { game.fail('hub', e); AIHub.fail(String((e && e.message) || e)); });\nelse game.start({ scenes, scene: 'title', boot });\n```\n- **Host authority** (`info.isHost`): the host copy runs `VersusSim`: NPCs, crew AI, bots, rules, scoring, the timer (from `AIHub.time()`).\n- **Every copy moves its own character locally** (no input lag) and reports it. The host trusts positions inside a speed check.\n- **Other copies render snapshots** with 150 ms interpolation.\n- **Messages:**\n  - Host → all, unreliable, 10 Hz: `{t:'s', at, tl, ev, cn, ph, p:[[id,x,y,f,st,fl],…], n:[[nid,x,y,f,st],…]}`.\n    - x, y are integers (world units); f is facing quantized 0-63; st is an animation code (0 idle, 1 walk, 2 run, 3 crouch, 4 watch, 5 act, 6 down, 7 sit, 8 phone, 9 bike); fl is flags (burned, carrying, shut).\n    - **Keep each message under 1,100 bytes.** If the NPC list makes it bigger, send NPCs in two alternating halves (5 Hz each).\n  - Host → all, reliable, on events: `{t:'e', k:'photo'|'burn'|'callout'|'tap'|'jump'|'seize'|'stash'|'reup'|'shut'|'open'|'sale', …}` (sounds, toasts, particles).\n  - Client → host, unreliable, 10 Hz: `{t:'me', at, x, y, f, st, w, ax, ay}` (w = watching; ax, ay = the aimed world point).\n  - Client → host, reliable: `{t:'act', k:'photo', target, q, at}` · `{k:'callout', target}` · `{k:'tap', phone}` · `{k:'jump', target}` · `{k:'shut'|'open'|'stash'|'reup'|'pickup'|'handoff'|'ditch', …}`.\n  - Budget: host 10 snapshots + under 5 events per second (< 20/s); clients 10 + a few.\n- **Validation on the host:**\n  - Positions are clamped to `maxSpeed × elapsed × 1.5` and to walkable cells.\n  - A photo claim needs: the target exists, is within 240 units of the claimer, and `map.sight` is clear on the host. If ★Act is claimed, the host's 1-second act history must show an act within 600 ms.\n  - Call-outs are checked against the true identity.\n- **Events:**\n  - `leave` → that player's character becomes a bot on the same side; the round goes on.\n  - The host leaving voids the round (the hub sends `end` `host-left`) → show \"Round cancelled\".\n  - `time-up` → the host finishes at once with the timer-end outcome.\n  - `end` → stop input (controllers to null), stop music and effects, show our results under the hub's. After `end`, send nothing.\n- **Server** (`AIHub.server`): the host has no local player. It runs the sim and shows a spectator camera (`cam.frame` over all players). No audio, no input.\n- **Finish** (host only, once):\n  - `standings`: winners `[[winning side ids],[losing side ids]]`; draw `[[all ids]]`. Use only ids from `AIHub.players` that haven't left (left players may be omitted: the hub places them last). Never bots, never the server's own id.\n  - `won`: when only one side has humans (one player vs bots, or every human on one side), pass `won: true` or `false` for that side. Standings alone can't say it.\n  - `scores`: `{ id: personalScore }`.\n  - `stats`: `{ id: { Photos, Burns, 'Call-outs', Count } }` (numbers or short strings).\n  - `awards`: `[{ id, title, detail }]`, e.g. `{ id: 2, title: 'Eyes On', detail: '4 hand-to-hand photos' }`.\n- **Session rules:**\n  - **Round length:** `min(300 s, info.limitMs − 5 s)`, so the host always finishes before the hub's hard limit (the harness sets it with `&limit=`).\n  - **Hidden host tab:** the engine's animation loop stops in a hidden tab. While `document.hidden` on the host, step the sim from a `setInterval` that calls `game.advance(elapsed)` (real elapsed time, capped at 1 s per call; browsers may throttle it to 1 Hz) and keep sending snapshots, so the other players don't freeze. Check that `advance` behaves while the loop is suspended. The hub voids the round after 20 s hidden anyway.\n  - **Names** from the hub are text: render them with `textContent` or canvas text (`E.font`), never as HTML.\n  - No other network use.\n  - No `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`, no new windows.\n  - Pause doesn't stop the host: the versus scene is `pausable: false` in a session. Options opens a non-pausing overlay with Settings/Controls (`game.menu('settings')`) while the character's input is suppressed.\n  - `menu.quit: false` and no restart items.\n  - While Create (Pad8) is held, ignore Options.\n- **Randomness:** the host never uses `Math.random` for the sim. Shared rolls come from `AIHub.rng(label)`, and the roster from `AIHub.rng('roster')`, so every copy builds identical NPC rigs.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Attract mode (52-attract.js)\n## 13. About/Credits screen and the exit interview\n- **`credits.json` is the single source** of the About pages and of `exit-interview.json`. The build copies its `exitInterview` object verbatim into `../exit-interview.json` and verifies it parses.\n- **About & Credits** (title menu → DOM screen; L1/R1 pages; large type; at most about 10 short lines per page; stick scrolls long answers):\n  1. **Credits:**\n     - WIRETAP; \"An original game inspired by *The Wire* (HBO, 2002-2008). Not affiliated with HBO or the show's creators.\"\n     - Design and code: the model(s) that built it (truthfully: if the builder isn't claude-opus-5-5 at max effort, list both, e.g. \"Planned by Anthropic claude-opus-5-5 (max); built by <id>\").\n     - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.1 by Michael Crosato (MIT). Multiplayer: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0.\n     - Any CC0 asset entries used (Kenney/KayKit via the engine's store), each with its source line.\n  2. **Source Information:** every field of TASK.md's list, same text as `exitInterview.sourceInformation`.\n  3. **Testing Feedback:** the 4 questions with answers.\n  4. **Engine Feedback:** the 6 questions with answers.\n  5. **Prompt Feedback:** the 4 questions with answers.\n- **Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.** Fill in real answers at the end of the build: no placeholders ship.\n- **Exit interview shape** (exactly TASK.md's). Suggested values to start from; correct any that turn out untrue:\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-03 10:53\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth investigation / crime drama with a corner-management sim and an asymmetric versus mode\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Isometric 2.5D (pixel-art 3D skeletons on a tile world); brawler-view interiors; HTML case-file screens\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.1 full engine (canvas 2D, optional WebGPU lighting); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; vanilla JavaScript; Web Audio\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"Engine-native retro-modern pixel art (PS1-era 2D look) pushed toward grit: sodium streetlight, rain, Formstone rowhouses, boarded vacants\",\n    \"animation\": \"Engine-native procedural Humanoid rigs (E.MOVES, poses, E.ACTIONS paired actions) plus custom joint post-processed poses; tweens and coroutine scripts\",\n    \"audio\": \"Code-written chip-synth music (engine music tracks, layered), synthesized effects and ambience, Web Audio formant gibberish voices with a telephone filter\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world versus for 1-4 players (host-authoritative); local split-screen versus; no other network\",\n    \"testing\": \"Lightweight, token-efficient testing as TASK.md allows (FPS targets and optimization out of scope). Run: <replace with what you actually ran, e.g. headless Node tests on the engine's fixed-step clock, engine check.mjs screenshots, Playwright gamepad/touch checks, the AI Hub harness, a viewport-matrix spot check>\",\n    \"research\": \"my-3D2dge handoff docs (API.md, AI_GUIDE.md, ASSETS.md, kits) and the AI Hub protocol README; knowledge of the series from memory; no other web research\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\",\n    \"usage\": \"unknown\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"…\", \"…\", \"…\", \"…\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"WIRETAP\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Somebody's always listening.\",\n    \"description\": \"Work both sides of a West Baltimore corner in 2004: build a wiretap case one photo, page and phone call at a time, then run the corner the case is built on. Includes a 1-4 player cops-vs-corner versus mode.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Stealth\",\n    \"source\": \"The Wire (2002-2008)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Stick/WASD move · L2/RMB watch · R2/LMB photo · ✕/E interact · △/Q partner · Create/Tab notebook\",\n    \"tags\": [\"story\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"isometric\", \"crime\", \"detective\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n- **The build's final chat reply** must also contain Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback (TASK.md).\n\n---","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013WaiYcVZi4NWX3Q5gLmtYE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
